(window.STEP_BOOK = window.STEP_BOOK || {})["R3"] = {"n":3,"level":"B1","t":"Reading Multi-Paragraph Texts","lessons":[{"id":"3.1","t":"Topic, Main Idea and Supporting Details","html":"<p><strong>Unit 3 · Lesson 3.1 · CEFR B1 · STEP link: Reading Comprehension (40%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Distinguish topic, main idea and supporting details.</p>\n<p>2. Identify the main idea of a numbered paragraph.</p>\n<p>3. Choose the best title for a multi-paragraph text.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يميّز الطالب بين الموضوع والفكرة الرئيسة والتفاصيل الداعمة.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يحدد الفكرة الرئيسة لفقرة مرقّمة.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٣. أن يختار أفضل عنوان لنص من عدة فقرات.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>The topic is what a text is about in one or two words (the Hejaz Railway). The main idea is what the writer SAYS about the topic in a full sentence (The Hejaz Railway changed travel to Madinah, but it was used for only a few years). Supporting details are the facts, numbers, examples and reasons that prove the main idea.</p>\n<p>At B1, STEP texts have numbered paragraphs, and you will be asked: What is the main idea of paragraph 3? Each paragraph has its own main idea, often in its first sentence (the topic sentence).</p>\n<p>The best title for a multi-paragraph text must cover ALL the paragraphs, not just the first one.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ What is the main idea of paragraph 2?</p>\n<p>▸ Which is the best title for the passage?</p>\n<p>▸ Which sentence best expresses the main idea of the passage?</p>\n<p>▸ Paragraph 3 mainly discusses …</p>\n<p>▸ Which detail supports the idea that …?</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الموضوع هو ما يدور حوله النص في كلمة أو كلمتين، والفكرة الرئيسة هي ما يقوله الكاتب عن الموضوع في جملة كاملة، والتفاصيل الداعمة هي الحقائق والأرقام والأمثلة التي تثبت الفكرة. وفي مستوى B1 تكون الفقرات مرقّمة، ولكل فقرة فكرة رئيسة غالبًا في جملتها الأولى، وأفضل عنوان يجب أن يغطي جميع الفقرات.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Read the first sentence of every paragraph and write a two-word note in the margin.</p>\n<p>2. Join your notes: this is the main idea of the whole passage.</p>\n<p>3. For a paragraph question, read the whole paragraph, not only the first sentence; sometimes the topic sentence is last.</p>\n<p>4. Check each option: Is it the umbrella idea, or just one supporting detail?</p>\n<p>5. For a title, choose the option that covers every paragraph.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اقرأ الجملة الأولى من كل فقرة واكتب ملاحظة من كلمتين، ثم اجمع الملاحظات لتكون الفكرة الرئيسة. وفي سؤال الفقرة اقرأها كاملة، وتأكد أن البديل فكرة شاملة لا تفصيلة داعمة، واختر العنوان الذي يغطي كل الفقرات.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ An option that is true but is only a supporting detail.</p>\n<p>✗ An option that is the main idea of a DIFFERENT paragraph.</p>\n<p>✗ A title that covers only the first paragraph.</p>\n<p>✗ An option that is too general (Transport in the World).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example text: Libraries on Wheels</strong></p>\n<p><em>(74 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]</strong> In some parts of Saudi Arabia, villages are far from the nearest public library. To solve this problem, several cities have started mobile libraries: buses filled with books that visit schools and villages every week.</p>\n<p><strong>[2]</strong> The buses carry about 3,000 books, from children's stories to science magazines. Each bus also has tablets with e-books and a small reading area with cushions. A librarian on board helps visitors choose books and organizes reading competitions.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. What is the main idea of paragraph 2?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. The buses carry 3,000 books.</p>\n<p>B. The mobile libraries offer many resources and services.</p>\n<p>C. Librarians organize competitions.</p>\n<p>D. Villages are far from libraries.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Paragraph 2 lists books, tablets, a reading area and a librarian.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> A and C are single details; D is paragraph 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is B.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 2. Which is the best title for the passage?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. Libraries on Wheels for Distant Villages</p>\n<p>B. Cushions in a Bus</p>\n<p>C. Science Magazines for Children</p>\n<p>D. The History of Saudi Cities</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Paragraph 1: why mobile libraries started; paragraph 2: what they offer.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Only A covers both.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is A.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">البدائل A وC تفاصيل داعمة، والبديل D فكرة الفقرة الأولى، فالفكرة الشاملة للفقرة الثانية هي B.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 B · Example 2 A","quiz":[{"l":["Read each text and answer the questions. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Text 1 (Questions 1–8): The history of the Hejaz Railway","(239 words)","[1] The Hejaz Railway was one of the most ambitious engineering projects in the Middle East. Built by the Ottoman Empire between 1900 and 1908, it connected Damascus in Syria with Madinah, a distance of about 1,300 kilometres. Its main aim was to help pilgrims travel to the holy cities more quickly and safely. The project was paid for mainly by donations from Muslims around the world, and thousands of workers laid the track across deserts and mountains.","[2] Before the railway, the journey from Damascus to Madinah took about forty days by camel caravan. Travellers faced extreme heat, a lack of water and attacks by bandits. The train reduced the journey to only four or five days, and the number of pilgrims increased greatly. Stations were built every 20 kilometres, with water tanks and small forts for protection.","[3] However, the railway's success did not last long. During the First World War, it became a military target, and large sections of the track were destroyed between 1916 and 1918. After the war, the line south of Ma'an in Jordan was never fully repaired.","[4] Today, the old station in Madinah has been restored as the Hejaz Railway Museum. Visitors can see original locomotives, carriages and photographs. Parts of the line in Jordan and Syria are still used for short tourist journeys. In recent years, there have been plans to reopen parts of the route, but no final decision has been made."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"Which is the best title for the passage?","o":["Camel Caravans in the Desert","The First World War in Jordan","The Rise and Fall of the Hejaz Railway","A Museum in Madinah"],"k":2,"e":"[best title] The passage covers building, success, destruction and today.","h":"العنوان الشامل."},{"n":2,"s":"What is the main idea of paragraph 2?","o":["Bandits attacked travellers.","The railway made the journey much shorter and easier.","Camel caravans were very slow.","Pilgrims need water."],"k":1,"e":"[main idea] Before: 40 days and danger; after: 4–5 days.","h":"فكرة الفقرة 2."},{"n":3,"s":"How long did it take to build the railway?","o":["about forty years","about eight years","about four years","about two years"],"k":1,"e":"[detail] between 1900 and 1908.","h":"ثماني سنوات."},{"n":4,"s":"Which detail supports the idea that the journey before the railway was difficult?","o":["The railway was 1,300 kilometres long.","The museum has old photographs.","The line in Jordan is used for tourists.","Travellers faced heat, lack of water and bandits."],"k":3,"e":"[detail] extreme heat, a lack of water and attacks by bandits.","h":"تفصيلة داعمة."},{"n":5,"s":"Paragraph 3 mainly discusses …","o":["how the railway was damaged and not repaired","why the railway was built","what visitors see in the museum","the life of pilgrims"],"k":0,"e":"[main idea] military target … destroyed … never fully repaired.","h":"الفقرة 3."},{"n":6,"s":"What was the main aim of the railway?","o":["to help pilgrims reach the holy cities","to carry soldiers to war","to attract tourists to Jordan","to transport camels"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] Its main aim was to help pilgrims travel to the holy cities.","h":"الهدف الرئيس."},{"n":7,"s":"The word ambitious in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["very old","very cheap","very large and difficult to achieve","very small"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] one of the most ambitious engineering projects → big and challenging.","h":"طموح."},{"n":8,"s":"What can visitors see in Madinah today?","o":["a working line to Damascus","a camel caravan","a new railway to Jordan","a museum with original trains"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] the old station … restored as the Hejaz Railway Museum … original locomotives.","h":"المتحف."}]},{"l":["Text 2 (Questions 9–16): How Red Sea coral reefs support fish","(242 words)","[1] The coral reefs of the Red Sea are among the richest ocean environments in the world. They stretch for more than 2,000 kilometres along the coasts of Saudi Arabia, Egypt and other countries, and they are home to over 1,200 species of fish. About one in ten of these species cannot be found anywhere else. Many visitors come to cities such as Jeddah and Umluj just to dive and see the colourful fish.","[2] Coral reefs support fish in several ways. First, they provide shelter: the holes and branches of the coral are perfect hiding places from bigger fish. Second, they provide food. Tiny plants and animals live on the coral, and small fish eat them. Third, many fish lay their eggs on the reef, so it is like a nursery for young fish.","[3] Red Sea corals are also unusually strong. Scientists have found that they can survive higher water temperatures than most corals in other seas. This may help them to survive as the oceans become warmer. Some scientists even call the northern Red Sea a possible safe place for corals in the future.","[4] Nevertheless, the reefs are in danger. Pollution, careless divers and fishing boats can break the coral, which grows only one or two centimetres a year. Saudi Arabia has created protected areas, where fishing and anchoring are not allowed, to protect this natural treasure. Visitors are also asked not to touch the coral or feed the fish."],"q":[{"n":9,"s":"What is the main idea of the passage?","o":["Fish lay eggs on coral.","Red Sea reefs are rich, important for fish and need protection.","Corals grow very slowly.","Divers break the coral."],"k":1,"e":"[main idea] Paragraphs: richness, support, strength, danger/protection.","h":"الفكرة الشاملة."},{"n":10,"s":"What is the main idea of paragraph 2?","o":["Big fish eat small fish.","Tiny plants live on coral.","Coral reefs give fish shelter, food and a place to breed.","Young fish need a nursery school."],"k":2,"e":"[main idea] First … Second … Third … three ways reefs support fish.","h":"فكرة الفقرة 2."},{"n":11,"s":"How many fish species live in the Red Sea reefs?","o":["about 2,000","over 1,200","about 120","exactly 10"],"k":1,"e":"[detail] home to over 1,200 species of fish.","h":"أكثر من 1200."},{"n":12,"s":"Why are reefs compared to a nursery?","o":["Children visit them.","They are very small.","Many fish lay their eggs there.","They are near the coast."],"k":2,"e":"[cause–effect] many fish lay their eggs on the reef, so it is like a nursery.","h":"مكان لصغار الأسماك."},{"n":13,"s":"Paragraph 3 mainly discusses …","o":["the dangers to the reefs","the number of fish species","the length of the coast","the special strength of Red Sea corals"],"k":3,"e":"[main idea] unusually strong … survive higher temperatures.","h":"الفقرة 3."},{"n":14,"s":"Which detail shows that broken coral takes a long time to recover?","o":["It lives in warm water.","It has holes and branches.","It grows only one or two centimetres a year.","It stretches for 2,000 kilometres."],"k":2,"e":"[detail] which grows only one or two centimetres a year.","h":"تفصيلة داعمة."},{"n":15,"s":"What is NOT allowed in the protected areas?","o":["fishing and anchoring","swimming","scientific research","taking photographs"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] where fishing and anchoring are not allowed.","h":"الممنوع."},{"n":16,"s":"Which is the best title for the passage?","o":["How to Dive Safely","The Red Sea's Coral Reefs: Rich but at Risk","Fishing Boats in Saudi Arabia","Why the Oceans Are Getting Warmer"],"k":1,"e":"[best title] Covers richness, importance and danger.","h":"العنوان الأفضل."}]},{"l":["Text 3 (Questions 17–23): The benefits of team sports for teenagers","(238 words)","[1] Team sports such as football, basketball and volleyball are popular with teenagers all over the world. While most people know that sport is good for physical health, research shows that team sports have many other benefits for young people. In Saudi Arabia, the number of school sports leagues has grown quickly in recent years.","[2] To begin with, team sports improve social skills. Players must communicate clearly, listen to the coach and support teammates who make mistakes. Teenagers who play team sports often find it easier to make friends and work in groups at school. They also learn to respect players from other teams, even when they lose.","[3] In addition, team sports can improve academic results. A study of 5,000 students in the United Kingdom found that those who played in a school team had slightly higher marks in mathematics and science. The researchers believe that sport teaches discipline and time management, which also help with studying. However, they warn that too much training can have the opposite effect if students do not leave enough time for homework.","[4] Finally, team sports help teenagers deal with stress and failure. Losing a match is disappointing, but it teaches young people that failure is a normal part of life and that hard work can lead to improvement. Coaches can help by praising effort, not only results. For these reasons, many schools now encourage every student to join at least one team."],"q":[{"n":17,"s":"What is the topic of the passage?","o":["team sports for teenagers","physical health","mathematics and science","school marks in the UK"],"k":0,"e":"[main idea] Every paragraph is about team sports and teenagers.","h":"الموضوع."},{"n":18,"s":"Which sentence best expresses the main idea of the passage?","o":["Football is more popular than volleyball.","Losing a match is disappointing.","Team sports have social, academic and emotional benefits for teenagers.","Coaches should listen to players."],"k":2,"e":"[main idea] Paragraphs 2–4: social, academic, stress/failure.","h":"الفكرة الرئيسة."},{"n":19,"s":"What is the main idea of paragraph 2?","o":["Coaches must speak clearly.","Teenagers make mistakes.","Group work is difficult.","Team sports help teenagers develop social skills."],"k":3,"e":"[main idea] To begin with, team sports improve social skills.","h":"فكرة الفقرة 2."},{"n":20,"s":"Which detail supports the idea that team sports improve academic results?","o":["Team players had slightly higher marks in maths and science.","Players listen to the coach.","Losing a match teaches about failure.","Many schools encourage students to join teams."],"k":0,"e":"[detail] A study of 5,000 students … slightly higher marks.","h":"التفصيلة الداعمة."},{"n":21,"s":"According to the researchers, why do team players get better marks?","o":["They have more free time.","Coaches help with homework.","They are taller and stronger.","Sport teaches discipline and time management."],"k":3,"e":"[cause–effect] sport teaches discipline and time management, which also help with studying.","h":"السبب."},{"n":22,"s":"Paragraph 4 mainly discusses …","o":["how team sports help with stress and failure","why schools encourage sport","the rules of team sports","the dangers of losing"],"k":0,"e":"[main idea] Finally, team sports help teenagers deal with stress and failure.","h":"الفقرة 4."},{"n":23,"s":"The word disappointing in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …","o":["very exciting","very tiring","making you feel sad because something went wrong","making you feel proud"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] Losing a match is disappointing.","h":"مخيّب للآمال."}]},{"l":["Text 4 (Questions 24–30): Traditional Saudi architecture and the summer heat","(233 words)","[1] Long before air conditioners existed, people in the Arabian Peninsula built houses that stayed cool in the extreme summer heat. Their methods were simple but very clever, and many architects today are studying them again. Some of these houses are more than two hundred years old and are still standing today.","[2] One important feature was the thickness of the walls. Houses in Najd, such as those in old Diriyah, were built with mud bricks and walls up to one metre thick. These walls absorbed heat slowly during the day and released it at night, so the rooms stayed cool in the afternoon. The mud also contained straw, which made the bricks stronger.","[3] Another feature was the central courtyard. Rooms opened onto an open space in the middle of the house, which allowed hot air to rise and escape while cooler air moved into the rooms. Small, high windows let in light but kept out direct sunlight. Families often slept in the courtyard or on the roof during the hottest months, because the air there was cooler at night.","[4] In coastal cities such as Jeddah, builders used wind towers and wooden rawashin to catch the sea breeze. Rawashin also gave families privacy, because people inside could see out, but people outside could not see in. Today, some new buildings in Riyadh combine these traditional ideas with modern materials to reduce the use of electricity."],"q":[{"n":24,"s":"Which is the best title for the passage?","o":["How Traditional Saudi Houses Stayed Cool","The History of Diriyah","Modern Air Conditioners","Windows in Jeddah"],"k":0,"e":"[best title] All paragraphs explain cooling methods.","h":"العنوان الأفضل."},{"n":25,"s":"What is the main idea of paragraph 2?","o":["Diriyah is an old town.","Thick mud walls helped keep houses cool.","Mud bricks are cheap.","Rooms were cool at night."],"k":1,"e":"[main idea] One important feature was the thickness of the walls.","h":"فكرة الفقرة 2."},{"n":26,"s":"How thick were some walls in Najd?","o":["about ten centimetres","two metres","half a metre exactly","up to one metre"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] walls up to one metre thick.","h":"حتى متر."},{"n":27,"s":"Why did houses have small, high windows?","o":["to let in light but keep out direct sunlight","to catch the sea breeze","to keep out bandits","to save money"],"k":0,"e":"[cause–effect] Small, high windows let in light but kept out direct sunlight.","h":"السبب."},{"n":28,"s":"Paragraph 3 mainly discusses …","o":["the materials used in Najd","wind towers in Jeddah","modern buildings in Riyadh","how the courtyard and windows helped cool the house"],"k":3,"e":"[main idea] central courtyard … windows.","h":"الفقرة 3."},{"n":29,"s":"The word absorbed in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["gave out","stopped","took in","reflected"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] absorbed heat slowly during the day and released it at night.","h":"امتصّ."},{"n":30,"s":"Why are some architects studying these methods again?","o":["They are very expensive.","They can help reduce the use of electricity.","Air conditioners are not allowed.","They are easy to build in one day."],"k":1,"e":"[cause–effect] combine these traditional ideas with modern materials to reduce the use of electricity.","h":"تقليل الكهرباء."}]}],"n":30},{"id":"3.2","t":"Making Simple Inferences","html":"<p><strong>Unit 3 · Lesson 3.2 · CEFR B1 · STEP link: Reading Comprehension (40%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Draw conclusions supported by evidence in the text.</p>\n<p>2. Reject options that go beyond the text.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يستنتج الطالب نتائج يدعمها دليل من النص.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يستبعد البدائل التي تتجاوز ما في النص.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>An inference is something the writer does not say directly, but which you can understand from clues in the text. If a text says Sara put on her coat and took her umbrella, you can infer that the weather was cold or rainy.</p>\n<p>STEP inference questions use words like infer, suggest, imply, probably and most likely: It can be inferred from paragraph 2 that … / The writer suggests that …</p>\n<p>A good inference is a SMALL step from the text. If an option needs a big jump, or adds information that the text does not support, it is wrong — even if it sounds sensible.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ It can be inferred from the passage that …</p>\n<p>▸ What does paragraph 3 suggest about …?</p>\n<p>▸ … was probably …</p>\n<p>▸ Which of the following is most likely true?</p>\n<p>▸ Why did … most likely …?</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاستنتاج معلومة لا يذكرها الكاتب صراحة لكنك تفهمها من القرائن، فإذا لبست سارة معطفها وأخذت مظلتها نستنتج أن الجو بارد أو ممطر. وتستخدم أسئلة الاستنتاج كلمات مثل infer وsuggest وimply وprobably. والاستنتاج الصحيح خطوة صغيرة من النص، أما البديل الذي يحتاج قفزة كبيرة أو يضيف معلومات غير مدعومة فهو خاطئ.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Find the part of the text the question is about.</p>\n<p>2. Collect the clues: actions, feelings, words such as suddenly, still, only, at last.</p>\n<p>3. Ask: What must be true if this is true?</p>\n<p>4. Reject options that are stated directly (that is a detail, not an inference) unless the question allows it.</p>\n<p>5. Reject options that are too strong (always, never, all) or that need information from outside the text.</p>\n<p>6. Choose the option you can prove with at least one clue.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حدد الجزء المقصود، واجمع القرائن من الأفعال والمشاعر والكلمات مثل still وonly وat last، واسأل: ما الذي يجب أن يكون صحيحًا إذا كان هذا صحيحًا؟ واستبعد البدائل المبالغ فيها مثل always وnever، أو التي تحتاج معلومات من خارج النص.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ The option is possible in real life but has no clue in the text.</p>\n<p>✗ The option is too strong (always, all, never).</p>\n<p>✗ The option repeats a fact from the text but answers a different question.</p>\n<p>✗ The option is the opposite of what the clues suggest.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example text: Before the Exam</strong></p>\n<p><em>(44 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]</strong> Faisal checked his bag for the third time: two pencils, an eraser, his calculator and his ID card. He had slept badly, and he could not eat his breakfast. When his mother wished him luck, he only nodded and looked at the clock again.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. It can be inferred that Faisal …</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. is nervous about the exam B. forgot his calculator</p>\n<p>C. did not study at all D. always fails exams</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Clues: checked his bag three times, slept badly, could not eat, looked at the clock.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> All clues point to nervousness.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> B is false; C and D have no evidence. The answer is A.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 2. Faisal's exam is probably …</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. in mathematics or a subject that needs a calculator</p>\n<p>B. a swimming test</p>\n<p>C. next month</p>\n<p>D. an art exam</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Clue: he packed a calculator.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The exam is today (he is checking his bag now).</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is A.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اجمع القرائن (التحقق ثلاث مرات، قلة النوم، عدم الأكل) لتستنتج أنه متوتر، واستبعد ما لا دليل عليه.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 A · Example 2 A","quiz":[{"l":["Read each text and answer the questions. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Text 1 (Questions 1–8): A new nurse's first week at a hospital","(224 words)","[1] When Maha started her first job as a nurse at King Khalid Hospital, she thought her four years at university had prepared her for everything. On her first morning, however, the head nurse gave her a list of twelve patients, and Maha's hands began to shake as she read it. She had never been responsible for so many people at the same time, and the ward was much busier than the hospital where she had trained as a student.","[2] The first few days were exhausting. She walked from room to room checking temperatures, giving medicine and writing notes. By the end of each twelve-hour shift, her feet ached so much that she fell asleep in her uniform on the sofa. On Tuesday, she forgot to record one patient's blood pressure, and the head nurse spoke to her quietly but firmly.","[3] On Wednesday, an elderly patient named Umm Saad held her hand and said, \"You remind me of my granddaughter. You always smile, even when you are tired.\" Maha went home that evening and called her mother to tell her the story.","[4] By Thursday, things were easier. Maha had learned the names of all her patients and the quickest way to the pharmacy. The head nurse told her she was making good progress, and Maha began to believe that she had chosen the right career."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 1 that Maha felt …","o":["bored with her new job","angry with the head nurse","confident about every task","nervous on her first morning"],"k":3,"e":"[inference] her hands began to shake as she read it.","h":"متوترة."},{"n":2,"s":"What does paragraph 2 suggest about a nurse's job?","o":["It is physically tiring.","It is boring and easy.","It is very well paid.","It only involves writing notes."],"k":0,"e":"[inference] exhausting … feet ached … fell asleep in her uniform.","h":"متعب جسديًا."},{"n":3,"s":"Why did the head nurse most likely speak to Maha on Tuesday?","o":["Maha had made a mistake with a patient's record.","Maha arrived late.","Maha wanted to leave the job.","Maha forgot her uniform."],"k":0,"e":"[inference] she forgot to record one patient's blood pressure.","h":"خطأ في السجل."},{"n":4,"s":"The word firmly in paragraph 2 suggests that the head nurse was …","o":["very angry and loud","joking","confused","serious and clear"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] quietly but firmly → serious but not shouting.","h":"بحزم."},{"n":5,"s":"Why did Maha most likely call her mother on Wednesday?","o":["She was happy about what the patient said.","She wanted to leave the hospital.","She was ill.","She needed money."],"k":0,"e":"[inference] Umm Saad's kind words → she wanted to share the happy story.","h":"كانت سعيدة."},{"n":6,"s":"It can be inferred that Umm Saad …","o":["was Maha's grandmother","liked and appreciated Maha","worked at the hospital","was angry with Maha"],"k":1,"e":"[inference] You remind me of my granddaughter. You always smile.","h":"تقدّرها."},{"n":7,"s":"How long were Maha's shifts?","o":["four hours","twelve hours","eight hours","twenty-four hours"],"k":1,"e":"[detail] By the end of each twelve-hour shift.","h":"اثنتا عشرة ساعة."},{"n":8,"s":"What can be inferred from the last paragraph?","o":["Maha will move to another hospital.","Maha no longer feels tired.","Maha is now more confident about her career.","Maha has become the head nurse."],"k":2,"e":"[inference] began to believe that she had chosen the right career.","h":"زادت ثقتها."}]},{"l":["Text 2 (Questions 9–16): A grandfather who repairs old radios","(218 words)","[1] In a narrow street in the old market of Al-Hofuf, there is a tiny shop with no sign above the door. Inside, shelves from the floor to the ceiling are filled with old radios: wooden ones, plastic ones, some with silver knobs and some with broken cases. Behind the counter sits Abu Fahad, who is seventy-four years old. He wears thick glasses and works slowly and carefully, with a small lamp and a box of tiny screwdrivers in front of him.","[2] Abu Fahad started repairing radios in 1968, when a radio was the most valuable thing in many homes. Families listened to the news, Quran recitation and football matches together. \"In those days,\" he says, \"if a radio stopped working, the whole street came to my shop.\"","[3] Today, most people listen to the radio on their phones, so his customers are different. Collectors bring him radios that they have bought online, and young people bring their grandparents' radios because they want to keep a family memory alive. Some customers drive from Riyadh, more than three hundred kilometres away.","[4] His grandson, Fahad, now spends every afternoon in the shop after university. He is studying electrical engineering, and he has started a social media page that shows his grandfather at work. The page already has more than 40,000 followers."],"q":[{"n":9,"s":"It can be inferred that Abu Fahad's shop …","o":["does not need a sign because people already know it","is new in the market","sells mostly mobile phones","is closed most of the time"],"k":0,"e":"[inference] no sign, yet customers come from Riyadh → it is well known.","h":"معروف."},{"n":10,"s":"What does paragraph 2 suggest about radios in 1968?","o":["They were very cheap.","Only rich families had them.","They often played music only.","They were very important in daily life."],"k":3,"e":"[inference] the most valuable thing … families listened together.","h":"مهمة جدًا."},{"n":11,"s":"What did families listen to on the radio in the past?","o":["only music and stories","only football","the news, Quran recitation and football matches","cooking programmes"],"k":2,"e":"[detail] listened to the news, Quran recitation and football matches.","h":"ما كانوا يستمعون إليه."},{"n":12,"s":"Why do young people bring their grandparents' radios?","o":["to sell them online","to listen to football","to learn engineering","to keep a family memory alive"],"k":3,"e":"[cause–effect] they want to keep a family memory alive.","h":"الحفاظ على الذكرى."},{"n":13,"s":"The fact that some customers drive from Riyadh suggests that …","o":["Riyadh has no shops","the radios are very cheap","Abu Fahad is moving to Riyadh","Abu Fahad has an excellent reputation"],"k":3,"e":"[inference] People travel 300 km → he is known as very good.","h":"سمعته ممتازة."},{"n":14,"s":"Fahad is most likely interested in the shop because …","o":["he wants to sell old radios online","his studies are connected to his grandfather's work","he does not like university","his grandfather pays him a lot"],"k":1,"e":"[inference] studying electrical engineering → related to radios.","h":"علاقة بدراسته."},{"n":15,"s":"The word valuable in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["heavy","noisy","modern","important and worth a lot"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] the most valuable thing in many homes.","h":"قيّم."},{"n":16,"s":"What can be inferred from the last sentence?","o":["Fahad wants to close the shop.","Many people are interested in Abu Fahad's work.","Abu Fahad uses social media every day.","The shop has 40,000 radios."],"k":1,"e":"[inference] more than 40,000 followers.","h":"اهتمام كبير."}]},{"l":["Text 3 (Questions 17–23): A student who changes her study habits","(212 words)","[1] For most of Grade 9, Lujain studied the night before every test. She would sit at her desk until one or two in the morning, reading her notes again and again with her phone next to her. Her marks were average, and she often felt too tired to concentrate in class the next day. Her parents told her many times to start earlier, but she always said that she worked better under pressure.","[2] In the second semester, her cousin Dana, a university student, noticed how stressed she was. Dana showed her a different method. Instead of reading the same page many times, Lujain began to test herself with flashcards. She also studied for thirty minutes every day, starting two weeks before each test, and she left her phone in the kitchen while she worked.","[3] At first, Lujain found the new method uncomfortable. Testing herself showed her how much she did not know, and she missed checking messages from her friends. But after a month, she noticed that she could remember facts more easily, even in the exam room.","[4] At the end of the year, Lujain's science teacher wrote on her report card: \"An impressive improvement.\" Lujain has since made flashcards for her younger brother, and she now goes to bed before eleven."],"q":[{"n":17,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 1 that Lujain's study method was …","o":["very successful","recommended by her teacher","not very effective","the same as Dana's"],"k":2,"e":"[inference] marks were average … too tired to concentrate.","h":"غير فعّالة."},{"n":18,"s":"What does the phone next to her in paragraph 1 suggest?","o":["She studied with her friends online.","She was often distracted while studying.","She used it as a calculator.","Her parents called her often."],"k":1,"e":"[inference] Later she leaves the phone in the kitchen → it was a distraction.","h":"تشتيت."},{"n":19,"s":"Dana noticed Lujain was stressed. Dana was most likely …","o":["Lujain's science teacher","younger than Lujain","experienced in studying successfully","not interested in school"],"k":2,"e":"[inference] a university student who showed a better method.","h":"لديها خبرة."},{"n":20,"s":"Why did Lujain find the new method uncomfortable at first?","o":["It showed her what she did not know, and she missed her messages.","It took eight hours every day.","Her teacher did not allow flashcards.","Her cousin was very strict."],"k":0,"e":"[cause–effect] showed her how much she did not know, and she missed checking messages.","h":"سبب عدم الارتياح."},{"n":21,"s":"What can be inferred about Lujain's science marks at the end of the year?","o":["They were exactly the same.","They were much better than before.","They were the lowest in the class.","They were not reported."],"k":1,"e":"[inference] An impressive improvement.","h":"تحسّنت كثيرًا."},{"n":22,"s":"The word impressive is closest in meaning to …","o":["very good and admirable","very small","surprising and bad","difficult to see"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] teacher's praise for her improvement.","h":"مثير للإعجاب."},{"n":23,"s":"Which of the following is most likely true about Lujain now?","o":["She still studies until 2 a.m.","She has stopped using flashcards.","She believes the new method works.","She does not help her family."],"k":2,"e":"[inference] made flashcards for her brother; goes to bed before eleven.","h":"تؤمن بالطريقة."}]},{"l":["Text 4 (Questions 24–30): A village gets electricity for the first time","(214 words)","[1] Until 2019, the small village of Al-Shuqaiq in the mountains had no connection to the national electricity network. The thirty families who lived there used small generators for a few hours each evening. The generators were noisy and expensive to run, and fuel had to be carried up a steep, rocky road. In winter, when rain damaged the road, the generators sometimes stopped for days, and the village was completely dark after sunset.","[2] Life followed the sun. Children did their homework before dark, and most families went to bed early. Food could not be kept for long, so the women cooked fresh meals every day, and meat was only eaten when an animal was slaughtered for a special occasion.","[3] When the power lines finally arrived, the whole village gathered in the square. As the first street lights came on, the children cheered and some of the older men were silent. Within a year, almost every home had a refrigerator, and the village school had computers and fans for the first time.","[4] Some things have not changed. Families still gather on rooftops in the evening to talk and drink tea. But the old generators now sit in a corner of the square, and the children who once studied by candlelight are now preparing for university."],"q":[{"n":24,"s":"It can be inferred that, before 2019, life in the village was …","o":["easy and comfortable","the same as in big cities","difficult in many ways","very noisy all day"],"k":2,"e":"[inference] generators noisy and expensive, fuel carried up a steep road, food could not be kept.","h":"صعبة."},{"n":25,"s":"What does Life followed the sun in paragraph 2 suggest?","o":["People worshipped the sun.","People's activities depended on daylight.","Families moved with the seasons.","Villagers worked only in summer."],"k":1,"e":"[inference] homework before dark … went to bed early.","h":"النشاط حسب ضوء النهار."},{"n":26,"s":"Why could food not be kept for long?","o":["The village was too hot.","The women did not like cooking.","Animals ate the food.","There were no refrigerators."],"k":3,"e":"[inference] Within a year, almost every home had a refrigerator → before, none.","h":"لا ثلاجات."},{"n":27,"s":"Why were some older men most likely silent when the lights came on?","o":["They did not like light.","They were deeply moved by the change.","They were asleep.","They were angry about the cost."],"k":1,"e":"[inference] A big, emotional moment; silence shows strong feeling.","h":"تأثروا."},{"n":28,"s":"What do the old generators in the square suggest?","o":["They are still used every evening.","They are for sale.","They are no longer needed.","They were never used."],"k":2,"e":"[inference] now sit in a corner of the square.","h":"لم تعد لازمة."},{"n":29,"s":"The word slaughtered in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["killed for food","bought at a market","raised on a farm","cooked slowly"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] meat was only eaten when an animal was slaughtered.","h":"ذُبح."},{"n":30,"s":"What can be inferred from the last sentence?","o":["Children no longer study.","Candles are now more expensive.","Everyone has left the village.","Electricity has improved educational opportunities."],"k":3,"e":"[inference] children who once studied by candlelight are now preparing for university.","h":"تحسّن التعليم."}]}],"n":30},{"id":"3.3","t":"Reference and Cohesion Across Paragraphs","html":"<p><strong>Unit 3 · Lesson 3.3 · CEFR B1 · STEP link: Reading Comprehension (40%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Track pronouns and this / these + noun across paragraphs.</p>\n<p>2. Answer The word X in paragraph 2 refers to … items.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يتتبع الطالب الضمائر وthis / these + اسم عبر الفقرات.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يجيب عن أسئلة The word X in paragraph 2 refers to …</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>At B1, reference words point further back — sometimes to the previous paragraph. Besides he, she, it and they, look for this, that, these, those, its, their, such, the former, the latter, one and ones.</p>\n<p>This and these often refer to a whole idea, not a single noun: Locusts can eat their own weight in food every day. This makes them very dangerous. (This = the fact that they eat so much.)</p>\n<p>Writers also use a different noun for the same thing (cohesion): the bicycle … the machine … the invention. Recognizing these chains helps you follow the passage.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ The word they in paragraph 2 refers to …</p>\n<p>▸ The phrase this problem in paragraph 3 refers to …</p>\n<p>▸ The word its in paragraph 1 refers to …</p>\n<p>▸ The phrase the latter refers to …</p>\n<p>▸ In paragraph 4, the word ones refers to …</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">في مستوى B1 قد تعود كلمة الإحالة إلى الفقرة السابقة، وإلى جانب he وshe وit وthey ابحث عن this وthese وits وtheir وthe former وthe latter وones. وكثيرًا ما تعود this على فكرة كاملة لا على اسم واحد، وقد يستخدم الكاتب اسمًا مختلفًا للشيء نفسه مثل the bicycle ثم the machine ثم the invention.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Find the reference word in the paragraph named in the question.</p>\n<p>2. Read the sentence before it — and if it starts a paragraph, read the end of the previous paragraph.</p>\n<p>3. Check grammar: singular or plural, person or thing, one noun or a whole idea.</p>\n<p>4. Replace the word with each option and read the sentence again.</p>\n<p>5. For the former / the latter: the former = the first of two; the latter = the second of two.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ابحث عن كلمة الإحالة في الفقرة المذكورة، واقرأ الجملة السابقة لها، وإن كانت في بداية الفقرة فاقرأ نهاية الفقرة السابقة. وتحقق من الإفراد والجمع ومن الشخص والشيء، ثم ضع البديل مكان الكلمة. وتذكر أن the former تعني الأول وthe latter تعني الثاني.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ Choosing the closest noun instead of the correct one.</p>\n<p>✗ Choosing a single noun when this refers to a whole idea.</p>\n<p>✗ Mixing up the former and the latter.</p>\n<p>✗ Ignoring singular and plural (its vs their).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example text: Two Kinds of Camels</strong></p>\n<p><em>(59 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]</strong> There are two main kinds of camels: the dromedary, which has one hump, and the Bactrian camel, which has two. The former lives mainly in the Middle East and North Africa, while the latter is found in the cold deserts of Central Asia.</p>\n<p><strong>[2]</strong> Both animals store fat in their humps. This allows them to travel for days without food.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. The phrase the latter in paragraph 1 refers to …</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. the dromedary B. the Bactrian camel</p>\n<p>C. North Africa D. the Middle East</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The two animals: the dromedary (first), the Bactrian camel (second).</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The latter = the second one.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is B.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 2. The word This in paragraph 2 refers to …</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. the cold deserts</p>\n<p>B. the fact that camels store fat in their humps</p>\n<p>C. travelling for days</p>\n<p>D. the Bactrian camel</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> This begins a new sentence and refers to the idea before it.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Storing fat allows them to travel without food.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is B.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">the former الأول وthe latter الثاني، وThis هنا تعود على فكرة تخزين الدهون كاملة.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 B · Example 2 B","quiz":[{"l":["Read each text and answer the questions. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Text 1 (Questions 1–8): The life cycle of the desert locust","(203 words)","[1] The desert locust is a type of grasshopper that lives in the dry areas of Africa, the Middle East and South Asia. Most of the time, it lives alone and causes few problems. However, under certain conditions, it can change its behaviour completely and become one of the most destructive insects on Earth.","[2] The life cycle of a locust has three stages: egg, hopper and adult. A female lays about eighty eggs in moist sand, usually after heavy rain. These hatch after about two weeks into wingless young locusts called hoppers. They grow quickly and shed their skin five times before they become winged adults about six weeks later.","[3] When rain produces a lot of green plants, the number of locusts rises fast. As they crowd together, they touch each other constantly, and this triggers a change: they become brighter in colour, more active and start to move in large groups. Such groups, called swarms, can contain billions of insects.","[4] A swarm can fly more than 100 kilometres in a single day and eat as much food as 35,000 people. For this reason, Saudi Arabia and its neighbours work together to monitor breeding areas and spray young locusts before they can fly."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"The word it in paragraph 1 (it can change its behaviour) refers to …","o":["the Middle East","the dry area","the desert locust","the problem"],"k":2,"e":"[reference] The desert locust … it lives alone … it can change.","h":"الجراد."},{"n":2,"s":"The word These in paragraph 2 refers to …","o":["the eggs","the females","the hoppers","the rains"],"k":0,"e":"[reference] lays about eighty eggs … These hatch after two weeks.","h":"البيض."},{"n":3,"s":"The word They in paragraph 2 (They grow quickly) refers to …","o":["the eggs","the adults","the females","the hoppers"],"k":3,"e":"[reference] young locusts called hoppers. They grow quickly.","h":"الحوريات."},{"n":4,"s":"The word this in paragraph 3 refers to …","o":["the green plants","the bright colour","the swarm","the locusts touching each other constantly"],"k":3,"e":"[reference] they touch each other constantly, and this triggers a change.","h":"التلامس المستمر."},{"n":5,"s":"The phrase Such groups in paragraph 3 refers to …","o":["groups of farmers","large groups of active locusts","green plants","eggs in the sand"],"k":1,"e":"[reference] start to move in large groups. Such groups, called swarms …","h":"الأسراب."},{"n":6,"s":"The phrase For this reason in paragraph 4 refers to …","o":["the eggs hatching in two weeks","the colour of the locusts","the great distance and damage of a swarm","the neighbours of Saudi Arabia"],"k":2,"e":"[reference] fly 100 km and eat as much food as 35,000 people → this is the reason.","h":"الضرر الكبير."},{"n":7,"s":"How many times do hoppers shed their skin?","o":["five","two","eighty","six"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] shed their skin five times.","h":"خمس مرات."},{"n":8,"s":"The word its in paragraph 4 refers to …","o":["the swarm's","the locust's","the food's","Saudi Arabia's"],"k":3,"e":"[reference] Saudi Arabia and its neighbours.","h":"تعود على السعودية."}]},{"l":["Text 2 (Questions 9–16): The growth of public libraries","(210 words)","[1] For most of history, books were rare and expensive, and only rich people, scholars and religious institutions owned them. In the Islamic world, great libraries such as the House of Wisdom in Baghdad collected thousands of manuscripts. However, these were mainly used by scholars, not by ordinary people.","[2] The idea of the public library, open to everyone without payment, developed much later. In the nineteenth century, cities in Britain and the United States began to build libraries paid for by taxes. Many of them were supported by wealthy donors who believed that knowledge should be available to all. One of these, Andrew Carnegie, helped to build more than 2,500 libraries around the world.","[3] In Saudi Arabia, public libraries have grown quickly in recent decades. The King Fahd National Library in Riyadh holds over a million items, and the King Abdulaziz Public Library offers reading programmes for children and adults. Its branch in Beijing is the first Saudi library outside the Kingdom.","[4] Today, libraries are changing again. Besides books, they offer free internet, study rooms, workshops and e-books that can be borrowed from home. Some people predicted that the internet would make libraries unnecessary, but the opposite has happened: they have become community centres as well as places for reading."],"q":[{"n":9,"s":"The word these in paragraph 1 refers to …","o":["rich people","the manuscripts in great libraries","ordinary people","religious institutions"],"k":1,"e":"[reference] collected thousands of manuscripts. However, these were mainly used by scholars.","h":"المخطوطات."},{"n":10,"s":"The phrase Many of them in paragraph 2 refers to …","o":["the taxes","British cities","the libraries built in the nineteenth century","wealthy donors"],"k":2,"e":"[reference] cities … began to build libraries … Many of them were supported by donors.","h":"المكتبات."},{"n":11,"s":"The phrase One of these in paragraph 2 refers to one of the …","o":["wealthy donors","libraries","cities","taxes"],"k":0,"e":"[reference] wealthy donors … One of these, Andrew Carnegie.","h":"المتبرعين."},{"n":12,"s":"The word Its in paragraph 3 refers to …","o":["the King Abdulaziz Public Library","the King Fahd National Library","Riyadh","the Kingdom"],"k":0,"e":"[reference] the King Abdulaziz Public Library offers … Its branch in Beijing.","h":"مكتبة الملك عبدالعزيز."},{"n":13,"s":"The word they in the last sentence refers to …","o":["people","e-books","libraries","workshops"],"k":2,"e":"[reference] they have become community centres → libraries.","h":"المكتبات."},{"n":14,"s":"What made public libraries different from earlier libraries?","o":["They had only religious books.","They were open to everyone without payment.","They were built by scholars.","They had no manuscripts."],"k":1,"e":"[detail] open to everyone without payment.","h":"مفتوحة للجميع."},{"n":15,"s":"The phrase the opposite in paragraph 4 refers to …","o":["the internet becoming slower","libraries closing","libraries becoming more important, not unnecessary","people stopping reading"],"k":2,"e":"[reference] predicted libraries would be unnecessary, but the opposite has happened.","h":"العكس."},{"n":16,"s":"How many libraries did Andrew Carnegie help to build?","o":["more than 2,500","about one million","about 250","exactly 1,900"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] more than 2,500 libraries.","h":"أكثر من 2500."}]},{"l":["Text 3 (Questions 17–23): Migratory birds crossing the Arabian Peninsula","(203 words)","[1] Twice a year, hundreds of millions of birds fly between Europe, Asia and Africa. Because the Arabian Peninsula lies between these continents, it is one of the most important routes for migratory birds in the world. Storks, eagles, swallows and many small songbirds pass over it every spring and autumn.","[2] Their journey is extremely dangerous. Small birds such as warblers weigh less than ten grams, yet they cross deserts and seas without stopping for hundreds of kilometres. To prepare, they eat so much before leaving that their weight almost doubles. This extra fat is the fuel for their flight.","[3] Large birds face a different challenge. Eagles and storks cannot flap their wings for long because it uses too much energy. Instead, they rise on columns of warm air and glide from one to the next. These cannot form over the sea, so the birds follow land routes, such as the coast of the Red Sea, and many cross at narrow points like Bab al-Mandab.","[4] Wetlands are vital resting places on the journey. Farasan Islands, for example, offer food and shelter to thousands of tired birds. Protecting such places is essential, since the loss of even one stopover can make the whole journey impossible."],"q":[{"n":17,"s":"The word it in paragraph 1 (pass over it) refers to …","o":["the world","the Arabian Peninsula","Europe","the spring"],"k":1,"e":"[reference] the Arabian Peninsula … pass over it.","h":"شبه الجزيرة."},{"n":18,"s":"The word Their in paragraph 2 refers to …","o":["the continents'","the seas'","the deserts'","the migratory birds'"],"k":3,"e":"[reference] Their journey → the birds' journey.","h":"الطيور."},{"n":19,"s":"The phrase This extra fat in paragraph 2 refers to …","o":["the food they find in the desert","the water in the sea","the fat in eagles","the weight the birds gain before leaving"],"k":3,"e":"[reference] their weight almost doubles. This extra fat is the fuel.","h":"الدهون الزائدة."},{"n":20,"s":"The word it in paragraph 3 (because it uses too much energy) refers to …","o":["flapping their wings for a long time","the warm air","the sea","the coast"],"k":0,"e":"[reference] cannot flap their wings for long because it uses too much energy.","h":"رفرفة الأجنحة."},{"n":21,"s":"The word These in paragraph 3 refers to …","o":["eagles and storks","land routes","narrow points","columns of warm air"],"k":3,"e":"[reference] columns of warm air … These cannot form over the sea.","h":"الأعمدة الهوائية."},{"n":22,"s":"Why do large birds follow land routes?","o":["They are afraid of water.","Warm air columns do not form over the sea.","They eat fish on the coast.","Land routes are shorter."],"k":1,"e":"[cause–effect] These cannot form over the sea, so the birds follow land routes.","h":"السبب."},{"n":23,"s":"The phrase such places in paragraph 4 refers to …","o":["wetland resting places","deserts","narrow points","large cities"],"k":0,"e":"[reference] Wetlands are vital resting places … Protecting such places.","h":"الأراضي الرطبة."}]},{"l":["Text 4 (Questions 24–30): The invention of the bicycle","(204 words)","[1] The bicycle is so common today that it is easy to forget that it was invented only about two hundred years ago. The first machine of this kind was built in 1817 by Karl Drais, a German inventor. It had two wheels and a wooden frame but no pedals. Riders sat on it and pushed themselves forward with their feet.","[2] In the 1860s, French makers added pedals to the front wheel. To go faster, later designers made this wheel bigger and bigger. The result was the penny-farthing, which had a front wheel up to 1.5 metres high and a tiny back one. It was fast, but it was also dangerous: riders often fell forward over the handlebars.","[3] The real breakthrough came in 1885, when John Kemp Starley in England designed the safety bicycle. It had two wheels of the same size and a chain that connected the pedals to the back wheel. Soon after, rubber tyres filled with air made riding much more comfortable.","[4] This design was so successful that modern bicycles look almost the same. Today, there are more than a billion bicycles in the world, and many cities are building cycle lanes to encourage people to use them instead of cars."],"q":[{"n":24,"s":"The phrase The first machine of this kind in paragraph 1 refers to the first …","o":["German car","wooden chair","inventor","bicycle"],"k":3,"e":"[reference] The bicycle … The first machine of this kind.","h":"أول دراجة."},{"n":25,"s":"The phrase this wheel in paragraph 2 refers to …","o":["the front wheel","the back wheel","the safety wheel","the rubber tyre"],"k":0,"e":"[reference] added pedals to the front wheel … made this wheel bigger.","h":"العجلة الأمامية."},{"n":26,"s":"The word one in paragraph 2 (a tiny back one) refers to …","o":["rider","pedal","wheel","metre"],"k":2,"e":"[reference] a front wheel … and a tiny back one (wheel).","h":"عجلة."},{"n":27,"s":"Why was the penny-farthing dangerous?","o":["It had no wheels.","It was too slow.","Riders often fell forward over the handlebars.","It had a chain that broke."],"k":2,"e":"[cause–effect] riders often fell forward over the handlebars.","h":"خطورتها."},{"n":28,"s":"The word It in paragraph 3 refers to …","o":["England","the penny-farthing","the chain","the safety bicycle"],"k":3,"e":"[reference] designed the safety bicycle. It had two wheels of the same size.","h":"الدراجة الآمنة."},{"n":29,"s":"The phrase This design in paragraph 4 refers to …","o":["Karl Drais's wooden machine","the safety bicycle with equal wheels, a chain and air tyres","the penny-farthing","modern cycle lanes"],"k":1,"e":"[reference] Paragraph 3 describes the safety bicycle; This design continues it.","h":"تصميم الدراجة الآمنة."},{"n":30,"s":"The word them in the last sentence refers to …","o":["cities","bicycles","cycle lanes","cars"],"k":1,"e":"[reference] encourage people to use them instead of cars → bicycles.","h":"الدراجات."}]}],"n":30},{"id":"3.4","t":"Words with Several Meanings","html":"<p><strong>Unit 3 · Lesson 3.4 · CEFR B1 · STEP link: Reading Comprehension (40%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Choose the meaning of a word with several meanings from context.</p>\n<p>2. Avoid the most common dictionary-meaning trap.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يختار الطالب معنى الكلمة متعددة المعاني من السياق.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يتجنب فخ المعنى الأشهر في القاموس.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>Many common English words have several meanings. Run can mean move fast on foot, but in He runs a restaurant it means manages. A dish can be a plate or a type of food. STEP loves these words because students often choose the first meaning they learned.</p>\n<p>The question usually says: The word X in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to … The options often include the common meaning as a trap, together with the correct meaning for THIS sentence.</p>\n<p>To decide, look at the words around it: the subject, the object and the topic of the paragraph. A restaurant is not something you can run with your legs!</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ The word run in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …</p>\n<p>▸ As used in paragraph 3, the word charge means …</p>\n<p>▸ In which sentence does the word … have the same meaning as in paragraph 2?</p>\n<p>▸ The word … could best be replaced by …</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كثير من الكلمات الإنجليزية لها أكثر من معنى؛ فكلمة run تعني يركض، لكنها في He runs a restaurant تعني يدير. ويحب اختبار STEP هذه الكلمات لأن الطلاب يختارون المعنى الأول الذي تعلموه. لذلك انظر إلى الكلمات المحيطة: الفاعل والمفعول وموضوع الفقرة.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Do NOT choose an option just because it is the meaning you know best.</p>\n<p>2. Read the full sentence and ask: What is the topic here (money, food, sport, nature)?</p>\n<p>3. Look at the words directly before and after: run + a business = manage; raise + money = collect.</p>\n<p>4. Replace the word with each option; only one will make sense.</p>\n<p>5. Check the meaning fits the whole paragraph, not just the sentence.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لا تختر المعنى الأشهر لمجرد أنك تعرفه، بل اقرأ الجملة كاملة وحدد موضوعها، وانظر إلى الكلمات قبلها وبعدها مثل run + a business = يدير وraise + money = يجمع، ثم ضع كل بديل مكان الكلمة.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ The most common dictionary meaning (run = move fast).</p>\n<p>✗ A meaning connected to the topic but not to the sentence.</p>\n<p>✗ A meaning from a different part of speech (a park vs to park).</p>\n<p>✗ An option that looks similar in spelling or sound.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example text: The Charity Match</strong></p>\n<p><em>(46 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]</strong> The school football match raised 8,000 riyals for a children's hospital. Fans paid a small fee to enter, and local shops donated prizes. At the end, the head teacher thanked everyone and said the money would be handed over in a short ceremony next week.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. The word raised in sentence 1 is closest in meaning to …</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. lifted up B. collected C. grew D. asked</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Raise + money = collect money.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Lifted up is the common meaning but does not fit money for a hospital.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is B.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 2. The word match in sentence 1 means …</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. a small stick for making fire B. a game between two teams</p>\n<p>C. something that looks the same D. a meeting</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Topic: football.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> A football match = a game.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is B.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">raise مع money تعني يجمع، وmatch مع football تعني مباراة، فالسياق يحدد المعنى.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 B · Example 2 B","quiz":[{"l":["Read each text and answer the questions. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Text 1 (Questions 1–8): A young chef runs a small restaurant","(209 words)","[1] At only twenty-four, Rayan Al-Ghamdi runs his own small restaurant in Al-Khobar. He opened it two years ago after training for three years in kitchens in Dubai and Istanbul. The restaurant has just twelve tables, and Rayan works there six days a week, often from early morning until after midnight. His parents were worried at first, because opening a restaurant is risky, but they now help him at weekends.","[2] The menu is short but creative. Rayan's most popular dish is a modern version of jareesh, made with crushed wheat, slow-cooked lamb and a light yoghurt sauce. He changes the menu every season, depending on which vegetables are fresh at the local market.","[3] Running a business is not only about cooking, Rayan explains. He has to order supplies, pay staff, keep the kitchen spotless and deal with customers' complaints. In his first year, he made several mistakes. Once, he did not order enough rice, and the restaurant ran out during a busy weekend.","[4] However, his hard work has paid off. Last month, a well-known food blogger gave the restaurant five stars, and now it is fully booked most evenings. Rayan hopes to open a second branch in Riyadh within three years, but he says he will never stop cooking himself."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"The word runs in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["moves quickly on foot","escapes from","flows","manages"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] runs his own small restaurant → manages it.","h":"يدير."},{"n":2,"s":"The word dish in paragraph 2 means …","o":["a plate used for serving","a satellite receiver","a bowl for washing","a prepared type of food"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] most popular dish … made with crushed wheat and lamb.","h":"طبق (طعام)."},{"n":3,"s":"The word light in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["bright","pale in colour","not heavy or rich","easy to carry"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] a light yoghurt sauce → not heavy.","h":"خفيف."},{"n":4,"s":"The word order in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["ask a supplier to send","tell someone what to do","put in a sequence","keep calm"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] order supplies → buy from suppliers.","h":"يطلب شراء."},{"n":5,"s":"The phrase ran out in paragraph 3 means …","o":["left the building quickly","became very busy","had none left","closed early"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] did not order enough rice, and the restaurant ran out.","h":"نفد."},{"n":6,"s":"The phrase paid off in paragraph 4 means …","o":["been paid back","been successful","cost a lot of money","stopped"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] his hard work has paid off → five stars, fully booked.","h":"أثمر."},{"n":7,"s":"Why does Rayan change the menu every season?","o":["because customers complain","to use the fresh vegetables available","because he travels to Dubai","to make the menu longer"],"k":1,"e":"[cause–effect] depending on which vegetables are fresh at the local market.","h":"الخضار الطازجة."},{"n":8,"s":"The word branch in paragraph 4 means …","o":["another shop or restaurant of the same business","part of a tree","a small river","a family group"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] open a second branch in Riyadh.","h":"فرع."}]},{"l":["Text 2 (Questions 9–16): Organizing a school charity bake sale","(209 words)","[1] Every year, Grade 8 at Al-Yasmin School holds a bake sale to raise money for a charity. This year, the students chose an organization that provides wheelchairs for children with disabilities. Their goal was to collect 5,000 riyals in one day.","[2] Planning started a month before the sale. The class was divided into four teams: baking, posters, money and set-up. The baking team asked parents to contribute cakes, cookies and maamoul, and they tested a few recipes themselves in the school kitchen. The poster team designed bright signs and put them up around the school and on the school's website.","[3] On the day of the sale, the students set up six tables in the main hall during the morning break. They decided to charge three riyals for a cookie and ten riyals for a slice of cake. The money team kept careful records and gave change to every customer. Within forty minutes, almost everything was sold.","[4] In the end, the students raised 6,350 riyals, more than their target. The head teacher matched that amount from the school fund, so the charity received 12,700 riyals. That was enough to buy three new wheelchairs. One of the wheelchair users visited the school to thank the students in person."],"q":[{"n":9,"s":"The word raise in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["lift up","grow","collect","increase the height of"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] raise money for a charity → collect.","h":"يجمع."},{"n":10,"s":"The word contribute in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["sell","eat","write","give as a share"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] asked parents to contribute cakes → give.","h":"يساهم."},{"n":11,"s":"The phrase put them up in paragraph 2 means …","o":["let them stay the night","hung them on walls","built them","increased their price"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] designed signs and put them up around the school.","h":"علّقها."},{"n":12,"s":"The word charge in paragraph 3 means …","o":["attack","ask as a price","fill with electricity","be responsible for"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] charge three riyals for a cookie.","h":"يتقاضى سعرًا."},{"n":13,"s":"The word change in paragraph 3 refers to …","o":["money given back when a customer pays too much","a difference","new clothes","a new plan"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] gave change to every customer.","h":"الباقي (الفكة)."},{"n":14,"s":"The word matched in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …","o":["played a game against","gave the same amount as","looked similar to","joined together with fire"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] The head teacher matched that amount, so 6,350 × 2 = 12,700.","h":"دفع مبلغًا مساويًا."},{"n":15,"s":"How much money did the charity receive in total?","o":["5,000 riyals","12,700 riyals","6,350 riyals","10,000 riyals"],"k":1,"e":"[detail] the charity received 12,700 riyals.","h":"المجموع."},{"n":16,"s":"What was the job of the poster team?","o":["to bake cakes and cookies","to keep records of the money","to set up the tables","to design signs and advertise the sale"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] The poster team designed bright signs and put them up.","h":"مهمة فريق الملصقات."}]},{"l":["Text 3 (Questions 17–23): The work of a national park ranger","(217 words)","[1] Saleh Al-Anazi has worked as a ranger in Asir National Park for eleven years. His job is to protect the park's wildlife, plants and visitors. He spends most of his day outdoors, walking along mountain tracks or driving to distant parts of the park in a four-wheel-drive vehicle. The park covers more than 4,000 square kilometres, so he often works with other rangers and keeps in contact by radio.","[2] One of his most important duties is to watch the Arabian leopard area, where the animals are being reintroduced. If he finds tracks in the mud, he takes photos and reports them to the research team. He also checks camera traps that record animals at night.","[3] Saleh also deals with visitors. Some people leave rubbish or light fires in areas where they are not permitted. When this happens, he explains the rules politely, but he can also give a fine. \"Most visitors are very cooperative,\" he says. \"They just need to understand why the rules exist.\"","[4] In spring, the park is crowded with families who come to see the green valleys and wild flowers. On busy days, Saleh may spend hours directing cars to parking spots. He says the best part of his job is seeing children notice a baboon or an eagle for the first time."],"q":[{"n":17,"s":"The word tracks in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["rough paths","marks left by feet","railway lines","pieces of music"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] walking along mountain tracks → paths.","h":"ممرات."},{"n":18,"s":"As used in paragraph 2, the word tracks means …","o":["mountain paths","songs on an album","marks left by an animal's feet","race courses"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] finds tracks in the mud → footprints.","h":"آثار أقدام."},{"n":19,"s":"The word light in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["not heavy","start (a fire)","bright","pale"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] light fires → start fires.","h":"يشعل."},{"n":20,"s":"The word fine in paragraph 3 means …","o":["very good","very thin","money paid as a punishment","sunny"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] he can also give a fine → a penalty.","h":"غرامة."},{"n":21,"s":"The word cooperative in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["willing to help and follow the rules","noisy","living together","afraid"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] Most visitors are very cooperative … they just need to understand.","h":"متعاون."},{"n":22,"s":"The word spots in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …","o":["small marks","places","pimples","short advertisements"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] parking spots → places to park.","h":"أماكن."},{"n":23,"s":"What does Saleh do when he finds leopard tracks?","o":["follows the leopard alone","closes the park","takes photos and reports them","calls the visitors"],"k":2,"e":"[detail] he takes photos and reports them to the research team.","h":"يصور ويبلّغ."}]},{"l":["Text 4 (Questions 24–30): Building a school robot team","(210 words)","[1] When Mr. Ibrahim, a physics teacher at Al-Rowad Secondary School, announced a new robotics club, he expected about ten students to apply. Instead, forty-five students signed up, so he had to hold a short test to choose the team members. In the end, he selected twelve students with different strengths: some were good at programming, others at building and design. The club met twice a week after school in the physics laboratory.","[2] The team's first project was a robot that could sort recycling into plastic, metal and paper. It took them three months. They faced many problems: motors that would not turn, sensors that gave the wrong readings, and a program with hundreds of lines of code that crashed again and again.","[3] In March, the team entered a national competition in Riyadh. Their robot had to complete a course in under two minutes. On the first attempt, it stopped in the middle because of a loose wire. With only five minutes before their final turn, the students fixed the problem.","[4] On their second attempt, the robot finished in one minute and forty seconds, and the team won second place. Mr. Ibrahim said he was proud not only of the result but of the way the students worked together under pressure."],"q":[{"n":24,"s":"The word apply in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["put on (cream)","use","ask to join","be relevant"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] expected about ten students to apply → ask to join.","h":"يتقدم بطلب."},{"n":25,"s":"The word strengths in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["muscles","power of a drink","number of people","skills and good qualities"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] different strengths: programming, building and design.","h":"نقاط القوة."},{"n":26,"s":"The word crashed in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["hit another car","fell down loudly","stopped working suddenly","lost money"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] a program … that crashed again and again.","h":"توقف فجأة (تعطّل)."},{"n":27,"s":"The word course in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["a series of lessons","a part of a meal","a direction of a ship","a route that must be completed"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] complete a course in under two minutes.","h":"مسار."},{"n":28,"s":"Why did the robot stop on the first attempt?","o":["There was a loose wire.","The motors were too slow.","The battery was empty.","The students forgot the code."],"k":0,"e":"[cause–effect] it stopped in the middle because of a loose wire.","h":"سلك مفكوك."},{"n":29,"s":"The phrase under pressure in paragraph 4 means …","o":["in a stressful situation with little time","below a heavy object","with a lot of air","very slowly"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] five minutes before their final turn.","h":"تحت الضغط."},{"n":30,"s":"How many students were selected for the team?","o":["ten","forty-five","five","twelve"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] he selected twelve students.","h":"اثنا عشر."}]}],"n":30},{"id":"3.5","t":"Writer's Purpose","html":"<p><strong>Unit 3 · Lesson 3.5 · CEFR B1 · STEP link: Reading Comprehension (40%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Identify why a writer wrote a text (inform, persuade, explain, entertain).</p>\n<p>2. Identify the purpose of an example or a detail.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يحدد الطالب سبب كتابة النص (الإخبار، الإقناع، الشرح، الإمتاع).</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يحدد الغرض من مثال أو تفصيلة.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>Writers write for a reason. To inform: give facts (a news report). To explain: show how or why something happens (a guide). To persuade: make readers agree or act (an advert, an opinion article). To entertain: tell an interesting story (a narrative).</p>\n<p>STEP asks about the purpose of the WHOLE text and also about the purpose of a part: Why does the writer mention …? The writer gives the example of … in order to … Paragraph 3 is included to …</p>\n<p>Look at the verbs in the options: to warn, to compare, to show, to criticize, to encourage, to describe. The correct verb matches what the paragraph DOES, not only what it is about.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ The main purpose of the passage is to …</p>\n<p>▸ Why does the writer mention …?</p>\n<p>▸ The writer gives the example of … in order to …</p>\n<p>▸ What is the purpose of paragraph 3?</p>\n<p>▸ The passage was most likely written for …</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يكتب الكاتب لسبب: الإخبار بحقائق، أو الشرح لكيفية حدوث شيء، أو الإقناع ليوافق القارئ أو يتصرف، أو الإمتاع بقصة شيقة. ويسأل الاختبار عن غرض النص كله وعن غرض جزء منه مثل: لماذا ذكر الكاتب كذا؟ ولماذا أورد هذا المثال؟ فركز على الفعل في البديل (to warn, to compare, to encourage) وهل يطابق ما تفعله الفقرة.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Ask: Is this mainly facts, instructions, opinions or a story?</p>\n<p>2. Look for persuasive language: should, must, imagine, you, the best, don't miss.</p>\n<p>3. For a detail question, read the sentence BEFORE the detail: the detail usually supports that idea.</p>\n<p>4. Match the verb in the option (to warn, to show, to compare) with what the writer is doing.</p>\n<p>5. Reject options that describe only one paragraph when the question asks about the whole text.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اسأل: هل النص حقائق أم تعليمات أم آراء أم قصة؟ وابحث عن لغة الإقناع مثل should وimagine وthe best، وفي سؤال الغرض من تفصيلة اقرأ الجملة التي قبلها لأن التفصيلة تدعمها، ثم طابق الفعل في البديل مع ما يفعله الكاتب.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ An option that describes the topic, not the purpose (to talk about museums).</p>\n<p>✗ Confusing to inform with to persuade when the text includes opinions.</p>\n<p>✗ Choosing the purpose of one paragraph for the whole text.</p>\n<p>✗ An option with a strong verb (to criticize) when the writer is neutral.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example text: Water Bottles</strong></p>\n<p><em>(65 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]</strong> Every year, Saudi Arabia uses billions of plastic water bottles, and most of them are thrown away after one use. A single bottle can take 450 years to break down. Imagine how different our beaches would look if every family used a refillable bottle instead. It costs less, it is better for the environment, and it only takes a small change in habit. Start today!</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. The main purpose of the text is to …</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. explain how plastic is made</p>\n<p>B. persuade readers to use refillable bottles</p>\n<p>C. describe Saudi beaches</p>\n<p>D. tell a story about a family</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Persuasive language: Imagine, It costs less, Start today!</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The writer wants readers to act.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is B.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 2. Why does the writer mention that a bottle can take 450 years to break down?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. to show how serious the problem is</p>\n<p>B. to compare plastic and glass</p>\n<p>C. to explain how bottles are recycled</p>\n<p>D. to describe the history of plastic</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The fact comes after the problem (bottles thrown away).</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> It makes the problem sound serious.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is A.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كلمات مثل Imagine وStart today تدل على الإقناع، والحقيقة الرقمية ذُكرت لتوضيح خطورة المشكلة.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 B · Example 2 A","quiz":[{"l":["Read each text and answer the questions. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Text 1 (Questions 1–8): A guide to saving energy at home","(212 words)","[1] Electricity bills in Saudi summers can be shockingly high, mainly because air conditioners run almost all day. The good news is that a few simple changes can reduce your bill by up to thirty percent without making your home uncomfortable. Here are five practical steps you can take.","[2] First, set your air conditioner to 24°C instead of 18°C. Each degree lower increases energy use by about six percent. Second, clean the AC filters every month; dusty filters make the machine work harder.","[3] Third, close curtains during the hottest hours of the day. Sunlight coming through windows can heat a room by several degrees. Fourth, replace old light bulbs with LED bulbs, which use about seventy-five percent less energy and last much longer.","[4] Finally, switch off devices at the wall instead of leaving them on standby. A television, a game console and a computer on standby can use as much electricity as a small fridge.","[5] The Al-Mutairi family in Riyadh tried these steps last summer. Their bill in August fell from 1,100 riyals to 760 riyals. If every household did the same, the country could save enormous amounts of energy and reduce pollution from power stations. Saving energy, in other words, is good for your wallet and for the planet."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"The main purpose of the passage is to …","o":["describe the history of air conditioners","complain about high electricity prices","explain how families can save energy at home","advertise a new kind of light bulb"],"k":2,"e":"[purpose] Here are five practical steps you can take.","h":"شرح طرق التوفير."},{"n":2,"s":"Why does the writer mention that each degree lower increases energy use by six percent?","o":["to explain how ACs are made","to warn people about cold weather","to show why 24°C is a better setting","to compare two brands of AC"],"k":2,"e":"[purpose] Set to 24°C instead of 18°C. Each degree lower increases energy use.","h":"تبرير الإعداد."},{"n":3,"s":"What does the comparison with a small fridge show?","o":["that fridges are very expensive","that televisions are bigger than fridges","how to switch off a fridge","how much electricity standby devices waste"],"k":3,"e":"[purpose] can use as much electricity as a small fridge.","h":"حجم الهدر."},{"n":4,"s":"Why does the writer include the example of the Al-Mutairi family?","o":["to describe a family in Riyadh","to prove that the steps really work","to show that August is hot","to sell LED bulbs"],"k":1,"e":"[purpose] Their bill fell from 1,100 to 760 riyals.","h":"إثبات الفاعلية."},{"n":5,"s":"According to the passage, LED bulbs …","o":["use much less energy and last longer","make rooms hotter","are used only on standby","are more expensive to run"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] use about seventy-five percent less energy and last much longer.","h":"مصابيح LED."},{"n":6,"s":"The passage was most likely written for …","o":["engineers who build power stations","students studying the history of electricity","shop owners who sell televisions","families who want lower electricity bills"],"k":3,"e":"[purpose] your bill … your home.","h":"الجمهور المستهدف."},{"n":7,"s":"What is the purpose of the last sentence?","o":["to give the sixth step","to explain how power stations work","to describe the Al-Mutairi family","to show the wider benefit for the country"],"k":3,"e":"[purpose] If every household did the same, the country could save energy.","h":"الفائدة الأوسع."},{"n":8,"s":"The word enormous in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to …","o":["very large","very small","dangerous","unknown"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] save enormous amounts of energy.","h":"ضخم."}]},{"l":["Text 2 (Questions 9–16): A review of a new science museum","(223 words)","[1] I visited the new Discovery Science Centre in Dammam last weekend with my two children, aged eight and twelve, and I can honestly say it is one of the best family attractions in the Eastern Province. It opened in January, and it is only fifteen minutes from the city centre by car.","[2] The centre has five floors, each with a different theme: the human body, space, energy, the ocean and inventions. Almost everything can be touched. My son spent forty minutes in the energy hall, where visitors pedal bicycles to light up a model city. My daughter loved the planetarium show, which is shown every hour in Arabic and English.","[3] The staff are friendly and well trained, and they clearly enjoy explaining the experiments. There is also a good café with healthy options, and the building is easy to move around with a pushchair or wheelchair.","[4] However, the centre is not perfect. Tickets are quite expensive at 95 riyals for adults and 60 for children, and the queue at the entrance on a Saturday morning took nearly half an hour. The ocean floor also felt small compared with the others.","[5] Overall, I would give the centre four stars out of five. My advice is to book online, arrive early on a weekday if you can, and plan to stay at least four hours."],"q":[{"n":9,"s":"The main purpose of the passage is to …","o":["explain how a planetarium works","give the writer's opinion of a science centre","advertise a café in Dammam","describe the history of science"],"k":1,"e":"[purpose] I can honestly say … I would give the centre four stars.","h":"مراجعة وتقييم."},{"n":10,"s":"Why does the writer mention her son spending forty minutes in the energy hall?","o":["to show how interesting the activities are for children","to complain that he was too slow","to explain how bicycles make electricity","to show that the hall was crowded"],"k":0,"e":"[purpose] Almost everything can be touched. My son spent forty minutes …","h":"إظهار التشويق."},{"n":11,"s":"What is the purpose of paragraph 4?","o":["to describe the five floors","to give advice about booking","to praise the staff","to mention the negative points of the centre"],"k":3,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function] However, the centre is not perfect.","h":"السلبيات."},{"n":12,"s":"Which of the following does the writer criticize?","o":["the friendly staff","the planetarium show","the high ticket prices","the healthy café"],"k":2,"e":"[detail] Tickets are quite expensive.","h":"ما انتقدته."},{"n":13,"s":"The passage was most likely written for …","o":["scientists who work in the centre","parents planning a family day out","engineers who designed the building","teachers who want to buy tickets in bulk"],"k":1,"e":"[purpose] my two children … family attractions.","h":"الجمهور."},{"n":14,"s":"Why does the writer suggest arriving early on a weekday?","o":["because tickets are free then","to avoid the long queue","because the café closes early","because the ocean floor opens only on weekdays"],"k":1,"e":"[inference] the queue on a Saturday morning took nearly half an hour.","h":"تجنب الطابور."},{"n":15,"s":"The word attractions in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["shops","schools","advertisements","places people enjoy visiting"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] one of the best family attractions.","h":"معالم جذب."},{"n":16,"s":"How many stars does the writer give the centre?","o":["five","four","three","two"],"k":1,"e":"[detail] four stars out of five.","h":"أربع نجوم."}]},{"l":["Text 3 (Questions 17–23): A lost hiker in the Sarawat Mountains","(216 words)","[1] Abdulrahman had walked the trail near Al-Baha many times, so he did not tell anyone where he was going that Friday afternoon. He took one bottle of water and his phone, which had only twenty percent battery, and set off at three o'clock, planning to be home before sunset. The weather was sunny and warm, and he did not think he needed anything else.","[2] An hour later, thick fog rolled into the valley. Within minutes, he could not see more than five metres ahead. He tried to follow the path, but the rocks all looked the same, and soon he realized he was lost. His phone showed no signal, and the temperature was dropping fast.","[3] Instead of panicking, Abdulrahman remembered advice from a mountain guide: stop moving when you are lost. He found shelter under a large rock, put on the thin jacket he had in his bag and switched off his phone to save the battery. Every hour, he turned it on for a minute to check for a signal.","[4] At nine o'clock, a weak signal appeared, and he managed to send his location to his brother. Rescue teams found him two hours later, cold but unhurt. Today, Abdulrahman tells every hiker the same thing: always tell someone your route, and never underestimate the mountains."],"q":[{"n":17,"s":"The main purpose of the passage is to …","o":["describe the history of Al-Baha","advertise a mountain guide","compare different mobile phones","tell a true story that teaches a lesson about hiking safety"],"k":3,"e":"[purpose] A story ending with a lesson for every hiker.","h":"قصة بعبرة."},{"n":18,"s":"Why does the writer mention that Abdulrahman had walked the trail many times?","o":["to show that the trail was easy","to prove he was a mountain guide","to describe the fog","to explain why he did not tell anyone where he was going"],"k":3,"e":"[purpose] had walked the trail many times, so he did not tell anyone.","h":"تفسير تصرفه."},{"n":19,"s":"What is the purpose of paragraph 2?","o":["to give advice to hikers","to describe how the situation became dangerous","to describe the rescue","to explain how fog forms"],"k":1,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function] fog … could not see … lost … no signal … temperature dropping.","h":"تصاعد الخطر."},{"n":20,"s":"Why did Abdulrahman switch off his phone?","o":["because it was broken","because he did not want to be found","to save the battery","to keep it warm"],"k":2,"e":"[cause–effect] switched off his phone to save the battery.","h":"توفير البطارية."},{"n":21,"s":"Why does the writer mention the mountain guide's advice?","o":["to show that Abdulrahman made a sensible decision","to criticize the guide","to explain why he got lost","to describe the guide's job"],"k":0,"e":"[purpose] Instead of panicking, he remembered advice … stop moving.","h":"إظهار القرار الحكيم."},{"n":22,"s":"The word underestimate in the last sentence means …","o":["climb something quickly","think something is less serious than it is","be afraid of something","measure the height of something"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] never underestimate the mountains → don't think they are easy.","h":"يستهين."},{"n":23,"s":"How long after sending his location was Abdulrahman found?","o":["two hours","one hour","six hours","twenty minutes"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] Rescue teams found him two hours later.","h":"ساعتان."}]},{"l":["Text 4 (Questions 24–30): An article that encourages daily reading","(213 words)","[1] How many minutes did you spend reading for pleasure yesterday? If your answer is zero, you are not alone. Surveys suggest that many teenagers now read less than ten minutes a day outside school. That is a pity, because daily reading is one of the easiest ways to become smarter, calmer and more successful.","[2] Reading regularly builds vocabulary faster than any word list. Researchers have estimated that a student who reads twenty minutes a day meets about 1.8 million words a year, while a student who reads five minutes meets only about 280,000. Over several years, the difference becomes huge.","[3] Reading is also good for your mind. A study at the University of Sussex found that just six minutes of reading reduced stress levels by more than two-thirds. It worked better than listening to music or going for a walk.","[4] You do not need to read difficult books. Comics, adventure stories, biographies and magazines all count. The important thing is to choose something you enjoy and make it a habit, for example, reading for twenty minutes before bed instead of looking at your phone.","[5] So tonight, put your phone in another room, pick up a book, and see where it takes you. You may be surprised how quickly twenty minutes passes."],"q":[{"n":24,"s":"The main purpose of the passage is to …","o":["report the results of one study","explain how to write a book","encourage teenagers to read every day","criticize teachers for giving homework"],"k":2,"e":"[purpose] So tonight, put your phone in another room, pick up a book.","h":"التشجيع على القراءة."},{"n":25,"s":"Why does the writer begin with a question?","o":["to make readers think about their own habits","to test readers' mathematics","to introduce a survey company","to show that reading is difficult"],"k":0,"e":"[purpose] How many minutes did you spend reading …?","h":"إشراك القارئ."},{"n":26,"s":"Why does the writer compare 1.8 million and 280,000 words?","o":["to show that word lists are useless","to show the big difference daily reading makes","to explain how researchers count","to prove that teenagers read too much"],"k":1,"e":"[purpose] Over several years, the difference becomes huge.","h":"إبراز الفرق."},{"n":27,"s":"What is the purpose of paragraph 3?","o":["to compare music and walking","to describe the University of Sussex","to show that reading reduces stress","to list different kinds of books"],"k":2,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function] six minutes of reading reduced stress levels.","h":"الفائدة النفسية."},{"n":28,"s":"Why does the writer mention comics and magazines?","o":["to show that any enjoyable reading is useful","to say that comics are better than books","to advertise a magazine","to warn against easy reading"],"k":0,"e":"[purpose] You do not need to read difficult books … all count.","h":"كل قراءة مفيدة."},{"n":29,"s":"According to the passage, how much reading reduced stress by more than two-thirds?","o":["six minutes","twenty minutes","ten minutes","one hour"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] just six minutes of reading reduced stress levels.","h":"ست دقائق."},{"n":30,"s":"The passage is most likely from …","o":["a scientific research paper","a dictionary","a magazine for young people","a library's opening hours notice"],"k":2,"e":"[purpose] Direct questions, you, and advice for teenagers.","h":"مجلة للشباب."}]}],"n":30},{"id":"3.6","t":"Fact vs Opinion","html":"<p><strong>Unit 3 · Lesson 3.6 · CEFR B1 · STEP link: Reading Comprehension (40%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Distinguish facts from opinions using signal words.</p>\n<p>2. Identify the writer's opinion on the topic.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يميّز الطالب بين الحقيقة والرأي باستخدام الكلمات الدالة.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يحدد رأي الكاتب في الموضوع.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>A fact can be checked and proved: The first electric car charging station in Riyadh opened in 2022. An opinion shows what someone thinks or feels; other people may disagree: Electric cars are the best choice for every family.</p>\n<p>Opinion signal words include: I think, I believe, in my view, should, must, best, worst, beautiful, terrible, unfortunately, surprisingly, it seems, probably. Facts often contain numbers, dates, names and research results.</p>\n<p>STEP asks: Which of the following is an opinion? Which statement is a fact? What is the writer's attitude towards …? To find the writer's opinion, look especially at the first and last paragraphs and at adjectives and adverbs.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ Which of the following is an OPINION rather than a fact?</p>\n<p>▸ Which of the following statements is a FACT?</p>\n<p>▸ The writer's attitude towards … can best be described as …</p>\n<p>▸ Which sentence shows the writer's opinion?</p>\n<p>▸ The writer would most likely agree that …</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحقيقة يمكن التحقق منها وإثباتها، أما الرأي فيعبر عما يفكر فيه شخص أو يشعر به وقد يختلف معه آخرون. ومن كلمات الرأي: I think وshould وbest وterrible وunfortunately. وتحتوي الحقائق غالبًا على أرقام وتواريخ وأسماء ونتائج أبحاث. ولمعرفة رأي الكاتب انظر إلى الفقرة الأولى والأخيرة وإلى الصفات والظروف.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Ask: Can this be checked in a book, a website or by measuring? If yes, it is a fact.</p>\n<p>2. Underline opinion words: should, best, terrible, I believe, unfortunately, wonderful.</p>\n<p>3. Remember: a sentence with a number can still be an opinion (Thirty minutes is far too long).</p>\n<p>4. For the writer's attitude, collect the adjectives and adverbs the writer uses.</p>\n<p>5. Separate the writer's opinion from opinions the writer reports (Some parents say …).</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اسأل: هل يمكن التحقق من هذه الجملة؟ إن كان نعم فهي حقيقة. وضع خطًا تحت كلمات الرأي، وتذكر أن وجود رقم لا يجعل الجملة حقيقة دائمًا، وفرّق بين رأي الكاتب وآراء الآخرين التي ينقلها.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ Thinking every sentence with a number is a fact.</p>\n<p>✗ Confusing a reported opinion (Some people think …) with the writer's own opinion.</p>\n<p>✗ Choosing a neutral option when the writer uses strong positive or negative words.</p>\n<p>✗ Thinking a strong statement is a fact because it sounds certain.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example text: Public Parks</strong></p>\n<p><em>(45 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]</strong> Riyadh has more than 400 public parks, according to the city's municipality. In my view, these parks are the most valuable places in the city. King Abdullah Park opened in 2014 and has a large dancing fountain. Every family should visit it at least once.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. Which of the following is an OPINION?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. Riyadh has more than 400 public parks.</p>\n<p>B. King Abdullah Park opened in 2014.</p>\n<p>C. These parks are the most valuable places in the city.</p>\n<p>D. The park has a large fountain.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> A, B and D can be checked.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> In my view and the most valuable show an opinion.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is C.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 2. The writer's attitude towards public parks is …</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. very positive B. neutral C. critical D. worried</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Words: most valuable, every family should visit.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> These are strong positive words.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is A.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عبارة In my view وكلمة should تدلان على الرأي، والأرقام والتواريخ القابلة للتحقق حقائق.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 C · Example 2 A","quiz":[{"l":["Read each text and answer the questions. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Text 1 (Questions 1–8): Electric cars on Saudi roads","(215 words)","[1] A few years ago, electric cars were almost never seen on Saudi roads. Today, the situation is changing quickly, and new models appear in showrooms every few months. In 2022, the Kingdom announced plans to make 30 percent of cars in Riyadh electric by 2030, and the first local electric car factory is being built in King Abdullah Economic City.","[2] Electric cars have clear advantages. They produce no exhaust gases, so they do not pollute the air in cities. They are also cheaper to run: charging a car at home costs much less than filling a tank with petrol. In my opinion, they are also more pleasant to drive, because they are quiet and smooth.","[3] However, there are challenges. Charging stations are still rare outside major cities, and a long drive between Riyadh and Jeddah needs careful planning. Some drivers also worry that batteries lose power in extreme heat. In addition, electric cars are still more expensive to buy than similar petrol cars.","[4] Personally, I believe electric cars are the future of transport in Saudi Arabia. The government should build more charging stations on highways as quickly as possible, especially on the long routes between the major cities. If it does, I am sure that most families will choose an electric car within ten years."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"Which of the following is a FACT?","o":["Electric cars are more pleasant to drive.","The Kingdom announced plans for 30 percent electric cars in Riyadh by 2030.","Electric cars are the future of transport.","Most families will choose an electric car."],"k":1,"e":"[fact / opinion] An announcement with a date and number can be checked.","h":"حقيقة."},{"n":2,"s":"Which of the following is an OPINION?","o":["Electric cars produce no exhaust gases.","A factory is being built in King Abdullah Economic City.","Charging stations are rare outside major cities.","Electric cars are more pleasant to drive."],"k":3,"e":"[fact / opinion] In my opinion, they are also more pleasant to drive.","h":"رأي."},{"n":3,"s":"Why do electric cars not pollute city air?","o":["They are quiet.","They produce no exhaust gases.","They are built locally.","They are charged at night."],"k":1,"e":"[cause–effect] They produce no exhaust gases, so they do not pollute the air.","h":"السبب."},{"n":4,"s":"Which challenge is mentioned in paragraph 3?","o":["There are few charging stations outside big cities.","Electric cars are too fast.","Petrol is too cheap.","Factories are closed."],"k":0,"e":"[detail] Charging stations are still rare outside major cities.","h":"التحدي."},{"n":5,"s":"The phrase Some drivers also worry … reports …","o":["the writer's own opinion","the opinion of other people","a scientific fact","a government plan"],"k":1,"e":"[fact / opinion] The writer reports other drivers' worries.","h":"رأي الآخرين."},{"n":6,"s":"Which sentence shows the writer's own opinion?","o":["The government should build more charging stations on highways.","Charging at home costs less than petrol.","Batteries can lose power in extreme heat.","Electric cars were rarely seen a few years ago."],"k":0,"e":"[fact / opinion] should = opinion/recommendation.","h":"رأي الكاتب."},{"n":7,"s":"The writer's attitude towards electric cars can best be described as …","o":["worried","neutral","enthusiastic","uninterested"],"k":2,"e":"[attitude / tone] the future … I am sure … more pleasant.","h":"متحمس."},{"n":8,"s":"The writer would most likely agree that …","o":["petrol cars are better for long trips","electric cars are too expensive to run","electric cars will become common in Saudi Arabia","the government should stop building factories"],"k":2,"e":"[attitude / tone] most families will choose an electric car within ten years.","h":"يتفق مع."}]},{"l":["Text 2 (Questions 9–16): Fast food and young people","(208 words)","[1] Fast food is everywhere in Saudi cities. Burger, pizza and fried chicken restaurants stay open late, and delivery apps bring meals to the door in less than thirty minutes, even after midnight. For many young people, eating fast food several times a week has become normal. Advertisements on social media, often featuring famous people, make these meals look exciting and cheap.","[2] Nutrition experts are concerned. A typical fast-food meal of a burger, fries and a large soft drink contains more than 1,200 calories, which is over half of what most teenagers need in a whole day. It also contains high levels of salt, sugar and fat. According to the Ministry of Health, about one in three Saudi children and teenagers is overweight.","[3] In my view, the problem is not fast food itself but how often we eat it. An occasional burger with friends is one of life's small pleasures. The real danger comes when fast food replaces home-cooked meals. Unfortunately, this is exactly what is happening in many busy households.","[4] Schools and families both have a role to play. Schools should teach students to read nutrition labels, and parents should cook with their children at weekends. Honestly, nothing tastes better than a meal you have made yourself."],"q":[{"n":9,"s":"Which of the following is a FACT?","o":["The problem is not fast food itself.","A typical burger meal contains more than 1,200 calories.","Nothing tastes better than a meal you made yourself.","An occasional burger is one of life's small pleasures."],"k":1,"e":"[fact / opinion] Calories can be measured.","h":"حقيقة."},{"n":10,"s":"Which of the following is an OPINION?","o":["An occasional burger with friends is one of life's small pleasures.","Delivery apps bring meals in under thirty minutes.","About one in three Saudi children is overweight.","Fast-food meals contain high levels of salt."],"k":0,"e":"[fact / opinion] life's small pleasures = personal feeling.","h":"رأي."},{"n":11,"s":"Where does the information about overweight children come from?","o":["the writer's friends","a fast-food company","a school","the Ministry of Health"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] According to the Ministry of Health.","h":"المصدر."},{"n":12,"s":"According to the writer, when does fast food become dangerous?","o":["when it is eaten with friends","when it replaces home-cooked meals","when it is delivered late","when it costs too much"],"k":1,"e":"[detail] The real danger comes when fast food replaces home-cooked meals.","h":"متى يصبح خطرًا."},{"n":13,"s":"The writer's attitude towards fast food is best described as …","o":["completely negative","completely positive","balanced: acceptable sometimes, harmful too often","uninterested"],"k":2,"e":"[attitude / tone] not fast food itself but how often.","h":"متوازن."},{"n":14,"s":"Which sentence shows the writer's opinion about schools?","o":["Fast food is everywhere in Saudi cities.","Schools should teach students to read nutrition labels.","Restaurants stay open late.","A meal contains over half of daily needs."],"k":1,"e":"[fact / opinion] should = opinion.","h":"رأي عن المدارس."},{"n":15,"s":"The word concerned in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["happy","busy","surprised","worried"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] Nutrition experts are concerned → worried about the numbers.","h":"قلق."},{"n":16,"s":"The word Honestly in the last sentence signals …","o":["a scientific fact","a number","a quotation from an expert","a personal opinion"],"k":3,"e":"[fact / opinion] Honestly, nothing tastes better … → personal view.","h":"رأي شخصي."}]},{"l":["Text 3 (Questions 17–23): Homeschooling around the world","(207 words)","[1] Homeschooling means that children are educated at home, usually by their parents, instead of attending a school. It is legal in many countries, including the United States, the United Kingdom and Canada. In some countries, families must register with the government and follow an official curriculum. In the United States alone, around three million children were homeschooled in 2021.","[2] Supporters say that homeschooling allows children to learn at their own speed and to study subjects that interest them deeply. Families can also travel more and spend more time together. Some parents choose it because their child was bullied or found a large classroom stressful. Others live far from a good school or travel often because of their work.","[3] Critics, on the other hand, argue that homeschooled children miss important chances to make friends and learn to work with people from different backgrounds. They also point out that not every parent has the knowledge or time to teach subjects like chemistry or advanced mathematics.","[4] I think both sides make good points. Homeschooling can work wonderfully for some families, but it is a serious responsibility. In my opinion, parents who choose it should make sure their children join sports clubs or community activities so that they are not isolated."],"q":[{"n":17,"s":"Which of the following is a FACT?","o":["Homeschooling can work wonderfully for some families.","Around three million children in the United States were homeschooled in 2021.","Homeschooling is a serious responsibility.","Parents should make sure their children join clubs."],"k":1,"e":"[fact / opinion] A number with a year can be checked.","h":"حقيقة."},{"n":18,"s":"Paragraph 2 mainly presents …","o":["the writer's own opinion","statistics about schools","the views of critics","the views of people who support homeschooling"],"k":3,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function] Supporters say that …","h":"آراء المؤيدين."},{"n":19,"s":"According to critics, what do homeschooled children miss?","o":["chances to make friends with different people","travel with their families","time to study their interests","help from their parents"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] miss important chances to make friends.","h":"ما يفوتهم."},{"n":20,"s":"Which statement shows the writer's own opinion?","o":["Homeschooling is legal in Canada.","Some parents choose it because of bullying.","Parents who choose homeschooling should make sure their children join clubs.","Not every parent can teach chemistry."],"k":2,"e":"[fact / opinion] In my opinion, parents … should make sure.","h":"رأي الكاتب."},{"n":21,"s":"The writer's attitude towards homeschooling is best described as …","o":["strongly against it","strongly in favour of it","confused","balanced"],"k":3,"e":"[attitude / tone] I think both sides make good points.","h":"متوازن."},{"n":22,"s":"The word isolated in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …","o":["very clever","happy","alone and cut off from others","busy"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] join clubs so that they are not isolated.","h":"معزول."},{"n":23,"s":"Why do some parents choose homeschooling?","o":["Schools are too far away.","Their child failed every test.","Their child was bullied or found big classes stressful.","Homeschooling is cheaper than travel."],"k":2,"e":"[cause–effect] because their child was bullied or found a large classroom stressful.","h":"السبب."}]},{"l":["Text 4 (Questions 24–30): Learning to code in primary school","(202 words)","[1] In 2014, England became one of the first countries to make computer coding part of the curriculum for children from the age of five. Since then, many other countries have followed. In Saudi Arabia, the Ministry of Education added computing and digital skills to primary schools as part of its plans for the future.","[2] At this age, children do not write long programs. Instead, they learn basic ideas such as giving instructions in the correct order, finding and fixing mistakes, and repeating steps. As they grow older, they move on to typing simple commands. Many lessons use simple tools where children drag colourful blocks to make a character move on a screen.","[3] I strongly believe this is one of the best changes in education in recent years. Coding teaches children to think logically and to keep trying when something does not work. Surprisingly, these skills also help in other subjects, such as mathematics and writing.","[4] Some teachers argue that young children already spend too much time in front of screens. This is a fair concern. However, many good coding activities can be done without a computer at all, using cards, toys or games in the playground. Every primary school should include them."],"q":[{"n":24,"s":"Which of the following is a FACT?","o":["This is one of the best changes in education.","Every primary school should include coding.","Coding is the most useful subject.","England added coding to the curriculum for five-year-olds in 2014."],"k":3,"e":"[fact / opinion] A date and a country can be checked.","h":"حقيقة."},{"n":25,"s":"Which of the following is an OPINION?","o":["Every primary school should include coding activities.","Children drag blocks to make a character move.","Saudi primary schools added digital skills.","Some coding activities use cards and toys."],"k":0,"e":"[fact / opinion] should = opinion.","h":"رأي."},{"n":26,"s":"What do young children learn in coding lessons?","o":["to give instructions in order and fix mistakes","to write very long programs","to build computers","to repair phones"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] giving instructions in the correct order, finding and fixing mistakes.","h":"ما يتعلمونه."},{"n":27,"s":"The word Surprisingly in paragraph 3 shows …","o":["a date","a scientific measurement","another person's opinion","the writer's feeling about a result"],"k":3,"e":"[fact / opinion] Surprisingly = writer's reaction.","h":"شعور الكاتب."},{"n":28,"s":"How does the writer respond to the concern about screen time?","o":["says the concern is completely wrong","agrees and says coding should be stopped","agrees it is fair but says many activities need no computer","ignores it"],"k":2,"e":"[attitude / tone] This is a fair concern. However, many … without a computer.","h":"رد الكاتب."},{"n":29,"s":"The writer's attitude towards teaching coding in primary schools is …","o":["strongly negative","neutral","strongly positive","uncertain"],"k":2,"e":"[attitude / tone] I strongly believe this is one of the best changes.","h":"إيجابي جدًا."},{"n":30,"s":"The word logically in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["in a clear, reasonable way","very quickly","with feelings","in a colourful way"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] think logically → reasonably, step by step.","h":"منطقيًا."}]}],"n":30}],"sc":{},"review":{"quiz":[{"l":["Unit Review — 50 questions on new texts. Read each text and choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","مراجعة الوحدة: خمسون سؤالًا على نصوص جديدة، اقرأ كل نص واختر الإجابة الصحيحة.","Text 1 (Questions 1–9): The Riyadh Metro project","(203 words)","[1] The Riyadh Metro is one of the largest public transport projects in the world. It has six lines with a total length of 176 kilometres and 85 stations, and it was built to serve a city of more than seven million people. The first lines opened to passengers in December 2024.","[2] Before the metro, almost everyone in Riyadh travelled by car. This caused heavy traffic, long journeys and air pollution. Planners hope that the metro will change people's habits. Trains run every few minutes, the carriages are air-conditioned, and there are separate sections for families and for single passengers. Its stations were designed by famous architects, and some, like the King Abdullah Financial District station, look like works of art.","[3] Building the metro was a huge challenge. Engineers had to dig tunnels under busy roads while keeping the city moving, and they had to protect the system from sand and extreme heat. Thousands of workers from many countries took part.","[4] In my view, the metro is the most important change to Riyadh in decades. However, it will only succeed if people can reach the stations easily. That is why buses connecting neighbourhoods to the stations are just as important as the trains themselves."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the main idea of paragraph 2?","o":["Riyadh has many famous architects.","Everyone in Riyadh used to travel by car.","Single passengers sit separately.","The metro was built to solve transport problems and offers comfortable travel."],"k":3,"e":"[main idea · 3.1] traffic problems before; frequent, comfortable trains now.","h":"فكرة الفقرة 2."},{"n":2,"s":"How many stations does the Riyadh Metro have?","o":["6","176","7","85"],"k":3,"e":"[detail · 3.1] six lines … and 85 stations.","h":"85 محطة."},{"n":3,"s":"The word This in paragraph 2 refers to …","o":["almost everyone travelling by car","the metro","the stations","the architects"],"k":0,"e":"[reference · 3.3] everyone travelled by car. This caused heavy traffic.","h":"الاعتماد على السيارة."},{"n":4,"s":"The word Its in paragraph 2 refers to …","o":["the city's","the carriage's","the family's","the metro's"],"k":3,"e":"[reference · 3.3] Its stations were designed by famous architects → the metro's.","h":"المترو."},{"n":5,"s":"The word lines in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["rows of words","train routes","queues of people","drawn marks"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context · 3.4] six lines with a total length of 176 km.","h":"خطوط القطار."},{"n":6,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 3 that building the metro …","o":["was technically difficult","was finished in one year","stopped all traffic in Riyadh","was done by local workers only"],"k":0,"e":"[inference · 3.2] huge challenge … tunnels under busy roads … sand and heat.","h":"صعب تقنيًا."},{"n":7,"s":"Which of the following is an OPINION?","o":["The metro is the most important change to Riyadh in decades.","The metro has six lines.","The first lines opened in December 2024.","Engineers dug tunnels under busy roads."],"k":0,"e":"[fact / opinion · 3.6] In my view …","h":"رأي."},{"n":8,"s":"Why does the writer mention buses in the last paragraph?","o":["to compare buses and trains","to complain about bus drivers","to explain how buses are built","to show that easy access to stations is necessary for success"],"k":3,"e":"[purpose · 3.5] it will only succeed if people can reach the stations easily.","h":"الغرض."},{"n":9,"s":"Which is the best title for the passage?","o":["Famous Architects of the World","Sand and Heat in Saudi Arabia","The Riyadh Metro: A Big Change for the Capital","How to Drive in Riyadh"],"k":2,"e":"[best title · 3.1] Covers size, purpose, construction and future.","h":"العنوان."}]},{"l":["Text 2 (Questions 10–18): Sea turtles nesting on Red Sea islands","(221 words)","[1] Every summer, on the quiet beaches of Red Sea islands such as those in the Farasan and Al-Wajh areas, an ancient event takes place. Female green turtles and hawksbill turtles crawl out of the sea at night to lay their eggs in the sand. Some of them have travelled hundreds of kilometres to return to the same beach where they themselves hatched decades earlier.","[2] A female digs a deep hole with her back flippers and lays about a hundred eggs. She covers them with sand and returns to the sea before sunrise. The eggs stay warm in the sand for about two months. Interestingly, the temperature decides whether the babies will be male or female: warmer sand produces more females.","[3] When the eggs hatch, the tiny turtles dig their way out and race towards the water, usually at night. This short journey is extremely dangerous. Crabs and seabirds catch many of them, and lights from buildings can confuse them so that they move away from the sea. Scientists estimate that only about one in a thousand survives to become an adult.","[4] To protect the turtles, Saudi Arabia has made several islands protected areas. Visitors are not allowed on nesting beaches at night, and lights near the coast must be kept low. These measures give the turtles a better chance of survival."],"q":[{"n":10,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["how sea turtles nest and why they need protection","the islands of the Red Sea","how crabs catch turtles","the life of seabirds"],"k":0,"e":"[main idea · 3.1] nesting, hatching, dangers, protection.","h":"الفكرة العامة."},{"n":11,"s":"The word them in paragraph 1 (Some of them) refers to …","o":["the female turtles","the islands","the beaches","the eggs"],"k":0,"e":"[reference · 3.3] Female turtles … Some of them have travelled.","h":"الإناث."},{"n":12,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 1 that turtles …","o":["always lay eggs during the day","can find their way back to the beach where they were born","live only on Red Sea islands","never travel far"],"k":1,"e":"[inference · 3.2] return to the same beach where they themselves hatched.","h":"يعرفون طريق العودة."},{"n":13,"s":"According to paragraph 2, what decides whether the babies are male or female?","o":["the temperature of the sand","the size of the egg","the depth of the hole","the time of night"],"k":0,"e":"[detail · 3.1] the temperature decides whether the babies will be male or female.","h":"الحرارة."},{"n":14,"s":"Why does the writer mention lights from buildings?","o":["to describe the islands at night","to explain one danger for baby turtles","to show that buildings are beautiful","to explain how turtles find food"],"k":1,"e":"[purpose · 3.5] lights … can confuse them so that they move away from the sea.","h":"خطر على الصغار."},{"n":15,"s":"The word race in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["a competition","a group of people","move very quickly","a type of turtle"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context · 3.4] race towards the water.","h":"يندفع."},{"n":16,"s":"The phrase These measures in paragraph 4 refers to …","o":["the dangers from crabs","the rules that protect nesting beaches","the temperatures of the sand","the number of eggs"],"k":1,"e":"[reference · 3.3] Visitors are not allowed … lights must be kept low. These measures …","h":"الإجراءات."},{"n":17,"s":"It can be inferred from the passage that if lights near the coast were brighter, …","o":["more turtles would lay eggs","fewer baby turtles would reach the sea","more males would hatch","crabs would leave the beach"],"k":1,"e":"[inference · 3.2] Lights confuse babies → they move away from the sea.","h":"استنتاج."},{"n":18,"s":"Which of the following is a FACT stated in the passage?","o":["Turtles are the most beautiful sea animals.","Every visitor should see turtles nesting.","A female lays about a hundred eggs.","The Red Sea is the best place for turtles."],"k":2,"e":"[fact / opinion · 3.6] about a hundred eggs → can be checked.","h":"حقيقة."}]},{"l":["Text 3 (Questions 19–26): How vaccines train the immune system","(219 words)","[1] Your body has a powerful defence system called the immune system. When harmful germs such as viruses or bacteria enter your body, special white blood cells attack them. Some of these cells also remember the germ, so if it returns, the body can fight it much faster. This is why people who have had certain diseases, like chickenpox, rarely catch them again.","[2] Vaccines use this natural memory. A vaccine contains a weak, dead or partial form of a germ, or instructions that teach cells to make a harmless piece of it. It cannot cause the real disease, but the immune system still recognizes it as an enemy and produces antibodies. If the real germ attacks later, the body is ready.","[3] Some people feel a sore arm or a mild fever for a day after a vaccine. This is normal; it shows that the immune system is working.","[4] Vaccines have saved millions of lives. Smallpox, a disease that once killed about three in ten of the people who caught it, was completely eliminated in 1980 thanks to vaccination. When most people in a community are vaccinated, germs cannot spread easily, which also protects babies and sick people who cannot receive some vaccines. Doctors call this community protection, and it is one of the strongest reasons to follow the vaccination schedule."],"q":[{"n":19,"s":"What is the main idea of paragraph 2?","o":["Vaccines prepare the immune system to fight a real germ.","Vaccines contain dead germs only.","Antibodies are white blood cells.","Germs can never return."],"k":0,"e":"[main idea · 3.1] A vaccine … the immune system recognizes it … the body is ready.","h":"فكرة الفقرة 2."},{"n":20,"s":"The word them in paragraph 1 (attack them) refers to …","o":["white blood cells","harmful germs","people","diseases like chickenpox"],"k":1,"e":"[reference · 3.3] harmful germs … white blood cells attack them.","h":"الجراثيم."},{"n":21,"s":"Why do people who had chickenpox rarely catch it again?","o":["The germ disappears from the world.","They take medicine every day.","Their immune cells remember the germ.","Chickenpox is not dangerous."],"k":2,"e":"[cause–effect · 3.1] cells also remember the germ … This is why …","h":"ذاكرة المناعة."},{"n":22,"s":"The word It in paragraph 2 (It cannot cause the real disease) refers to …","o":["the immune system","the vaccine","the real germ","the body"],"k":1,"e":"[reference · 3.3] A vaccine contains … It cannot cause the real disease.","h":"اللقاح."},{"n":23,"s":"What does paragraph 3 suggest about a mild fever after a vaccine?","o":["It means the vaccine failed.","It always lasts a week.","It is not a reason to worry.","It is caused by the real disease."],"k":2,"e":"[inference · 3.2] This is normal; it shows that the immune system is working.","h":"لا داعي للقلق."},{"n":24,"s":"Why does the writer mention smallpox?","o":["to give an example of a disease defeated by vaccines","to describe how smallpox spreads","to warn that smallpox is returning","to compare smallpox and chickenpox"],"k":0,"e":"[purpose · 3.5] completely eliminated in 1980 thanks to vaccination.","h":"مثال للنجاح."},{"n":25,"s":"The word eliminated in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …","o":["discovered","studied","reduced a little","completely removed"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context · 3.4] completely eliminated → no longer exists.","h":"قُضي عليه."},{"n":26,"s":"It can be inferred that community protection is especially important for …","o":["doctors and nurses only","people who cannot receive some vaccines","people who already had smallpox","germs"],"k":1,"e":"[inference · 3.2] protects babies and sick people who cannot receive some vaccines.","h":"الفئات الضعيفة."}]},{"l":["Text 4 (Questions 27–34): The travels of Ibn Battuta","(211 words)","[1] In 1325, a twenty-one-year-old man named Ibn Battuta left his home in Tangier, Morocco, to perform Hajj in Makkah. He expected to be away for about a year and a half. Instead, he travelled for nearly thirty years and covered more than 117,000 kilometres, more than any known traveller before the age of steam.","[2] After Hajj, he decided to see as much of the world as possible. He crossed Arabia, sailed down the coast of East Africa, and travelled through Persia, Anatolia and Central Asia. In India, the sultan of Delhi employed him as a judge for several years. Later, he visited the Maldives, China and the Malay Peninsula before returning home.","[3] His journeys were often dangerous. He survived storms at sea, attacks by robbers and serious illness. Several times he lost all his possessions. Yet he continued, partly because rulers and scholars welcomed him generously wherever Arabic and Islamic learning were respected.","[4] When he finally returned to Morocco, the sultan asked a scholar, Ibn Juzayy, to write down his stories. The result, known as the Rihla, is a remarkable record of the fourteenth-century world. Today, it is one of the most valuable sources for historians, and a large shopping centre in Dubai is even named after this extraordinary traveller."],"q":[{"n":27,"s":"Which is the best title for the passage?","o":["Ibn Battuta: The Traveller of the Medieval World","The History of Tangier","How to Perform Hajj","Storms at Sea"],"k":0,"e":"[best title · 3.1] Covers his travels, dangers and book.","h":"العنوان."},{"n":28,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 1 that Ibn Battuta …","o":["wanted to become a sultan","travelled by steam ship","never reached Makkah","did not originally plan such a long journey"],"k":3,"e":"[inference · 3.2] expected a year and a half … travelled nearly thirty years.","h":"لم يخطط لذلك."},{"n":29,"s":"The word him in paragraph 2 refers to …","o":["the sultan of Delhi","a judge","Ibn Battuta","a scholar"],"k":2,"e":"[reference · 3.3] the sultan employed him as a judge → Ibn Battuta.","h":"ابن بطوطة."},{"n":30,"s":"What was Ibn Battuta's job in India?","o":["a sailor","a merchant","a teacher of medicine","a judge"],"k":3,"e":"[detail · 3.1] employed him as a judge.","h":"قاضٍ."},{"n":31,"s":"Why does the writer mention storms, robbers and illness?","o":["to explain why he went home early","to describe life in Morocco","to criticize the rulers","to show how dangerous his journeys were"],"k":3,"e":"[purpose · 3.5] His journeys were often dangerous.","h":"خطورة الرحلات."},{"n":32,"s":"The word record in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …","o":["the best result ever","a music disc","a written account","to save on a device"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context · 3.4] a remarkable record of the fourteenth-century world.","h":"سجل مكتوب."},{"n":33,"s":"Which of the following is an OPINION?","o":["He left Tangier in 1325.","Ibn Battuta was an extraordinary traveller.","Ibn Juzayy wrote down his stories.","He visited the Maldives."],"k":1,"e":"[fact / opinion · 3.6] extraordinary = judgment.","h":"رأي."},{"n":34,"s":"The phrase The result in paragraph 4 refers to …","o":["his return to Morocco","a shopping centre","the book of his stories written by Ibn Juzayy","a sultan's order"],"k":2,"e":"[reference · 3.3] asked a scholar to write down his stories. The result, known as the Rihla.","h":"الكتاب."}]},{"l":["Text 5 (Questions 35–42): Why people collect stamps","(206 words)","[1] Stamp collecting, or philately, has been a popular hobby since the first postage stamp, the Penny Black, was issued in Britain in 1840. Today, millions of people around the world collect stamps, from young children to famous people. King Abdulaziz's sons, for example, were among the early collectors of Saudi stamps.","[2] Why do people collect these small pieces of paper? For many, stamps are like tiny windows into the world. A single album can show the animals, buildings, leaders and festivals of dozens of countries. Collectors often say that they learned more geography and history from stamps than from school textbooks.","[3] Others enjoy the challenge of completing a set or finding a rare stamp. Some old stamps with printing mistakes are worth enormous sums. In 2021, a single stamp from British Guiana was sold for more than eight million dollars. Of course, most collections are worth very little money, but that is not why most people collect.","[4] I personally think stamp collecting is a wonderful hobby for young people. It teaches patience, care and organization, and it does not require a screen. However, since fewer people send letters today, collectors now often buy stamps directly from post offices or online, rather than finding them on envelopes."],"q":[{"n":35,"s":"What is the main idea of paragraph 2?","o":["Stamps help collectors learn about the world.","Albums are expensive.","School textbooks are boring.","Every country prints animals on stamps."],"k":0,"e":"[main idea · 3.1] tiny windows into the world … geography and history.","h":"فكرة الفقرة 2."},{"n":36,"s":"The phrase these small pieces of paper in paragraph 2 refers to …","o":["letters","albums","stamps","textbooks"],"k":2,"e":"[reference · 3.3] Why do people collect these small pieces of paper? → stamps.","h":"الطوابع."},{"n":37,"s":"The word issued in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["discussed as a problem","lost","officially produced and made available","copied"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context · 3.4] the Penny Black was issued in 1840.","h":"أُصدر."},{"n":38,"s":"Why does the writer mention the stamp from British Guiana?","o":["to explain the history of Guiana","to advise readers to sell stamps","to show that stamps are cheap","to show that some rare stamps are worth a lot of money"],"k":3,"e":"[purpose · 3.5] sold for more than eight million dollars.","h":"قيمة الطوابع النادرة."},{"n":39,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 3 that most people collect stamps …","o":["for enjoyment rather than money","to become rich","because their school asks them","to send letters"],"k":0,"e":"[inference · 3.2] most collections are worth very little money, but that is not why most people collect.","h":"للمتعة."},{"n":40,"s":"Which of the following is an OPINION?","o":["The Penny Black was issued in 1840.","A stamp was sold for over eight million dollars.","Collectors buy stamps online.","Stamp collecting is a wonderful hobby for young people."],"k":3,"e":"[fact / opinion · 3.6] I personally think … wonderful.","h":"رأي."},{"n":41,"s":"The word set in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["to put something down","a group of things that belong together","ready","a television"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context · 3.4] completing a set → a complete group of stamps.","h":"مجموعة."},{"n":42,"s":"Why do collectors now often buy stamps from post offices or online?","o":["Stamps on envelopes are fake.","Fewer people send letters.","Post offices give stamps for free.","Online stamps are always rare."],"k":1,"e":"[cause–effect · 3.2] since fewer people send letters today.","h":"السبب."}]},{"l":["Text 6 (Questions 43–50): Wildfires and how to prevent them","(203 words)","[1] Wildfires are uncontrolled fires that spread through forests, grasslands or bushland. In recent years, huge wildfires in countries such as Australia, Canada and Greece have destroyed millions of hectares, burned thousands of homes and filled the air with smoke for weeks. In Saudi Arabia, fires sometimes break out in the juniper forests of the southern mountains.","[2] Three things are needed for a fire: heat, fuel and oxygen. In hot, dry and windy weather, dead leaves and dry grass catch fire very easily, and the wind carries burning pieces to new areas. Scientists say that rising temperatures are making fire seasons longer and more intense.","[3] Surprisingly, most wildfires are not started by nature. Lightning causes some, but experts estimate that around eighty-five percent are caused by people. Campfires left burning, cigarettes thrown from cars and sparks from machines are common causes.","[4] Fortunately, many fires can be prevented. Campers should use fireplaces where they are provided and pour water on the fire until it is completely cold. Rubbish should never be burned outdoors on windy days. Authorities can also help by clearing dry plants near roads and villages and by closing high-risk areas during heatwaves. Everyone who enjoys nature has a duty to protect it."],"q":[{"n":43,"s":"Which is the best title for the passage?","o":["The Forests of Australia","How Lightning Works","Camping in the Mountains","Wildfires: Causes and Prevention"],"k":3,"e":"[best title · 3.1] Covers what wildfires are, how they spread, causes and prevention.","h":"العنوان."},{"n":44,"s":"The word fuel in paragraph 2 refers to …","o":["petrol for cars","wind","material that burns, such as dry leaves and grass","oxygen"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context · 3.4] Three things: heat, fuel and oxygen … dead leaves and dry grass catch fire.","h":"الوقود (المواد القابلة للاشتعال)."},{"n":45,"s":"The word Surprisingly in paragraph 3 suggests that the writer thinks …","o":["many readers would expect nature to cause most fires","lightning is very rare","people never start fires","fires are not dangerous"],"k":0,"e":"[inference · 3.2] Surprisingly, most wildfires are not started by nature.","h":"مفاجأة للقارئ."},{"n":46,"s":"What percentage of wildfires are caused by people, according to experts?","o":["around fifteen percent","around fifty percent","around eighty-five percent","all of them"],"k":2,"e":"[detail · 3.1] around eighty-five percent are caused by people.","h":"85%."},{"n":47,"s":"The word it in paragraph 4 (until it is completely cold) refers to …","o":["the water","the fireplace","the campfire","the wind"],"k":2,"e":"[reference · 3.3] pour water on the fire until it is completely cold.","h":"النار."},{"n":48,"s":"What is the purpose of paragraph 4?","o":["to explain how wildfires can be prevented","to describe the damage in Greece","to explain what fire needs","to list the causes of lightning"],"k":0,"e":"[purpose · 3.5] Fortunately, many fires can be prevented.","h":"الوقاية."},{"n":49,"s":"Which of the following is an OPINION?","o":["Fires need heat, fuel and oxygen.","Lightning causes some wildfires.","Fires broke out in Australia, Canada and Greece.","Everyone who enjoys nature has a duty to protect it."],"k":3,"e":"[fact / opinion · 3.6] has a duty = writer's judgment.","h":"رأي."},{"n":50,"s":"The word intense in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["short","strong and extreme","cool","rare"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context · 3.4] longer and more intense fire seasons.","h":"شديد."}]}],"n":50}};
